**TRAINING ASSISTANCE**

4.	Nil.

- **TRAINING SUPPORT**

5.	In the classroom/computer lab area:

		- sufficient tables and chairs for 14 persons (4 instructors, 8 candidates, 2 visitors/Stds representative);

		- whiteboard;

		- clock;

		- pointer (laser or otherwise);

		- nine workstations with hardware and software applications to support multiple Virtual Machines (VMs), to include Intel processors with VT X support and support for two-monitor display;

		- Windows and Linux VMs;

		- one network switch (min 12 port);

		- one Microsoft Develop Network (MSDN) licence;

		- enterprise antivirus, secure information and event management (SIEM), and Intrusion Detection System (IDS)/Intrusion Prevention System (IPS) software;

		- Cyber range hardware and software (VCentre, ESXi virtualization suite, server);

		- nine attack framework installations (Metasploit, Cobalt Strike, Empire, etc.);

		- nine applicable licences for each attack tool as required;

		- nine MS Office suite licences;

		- target vitual machines that simulate an enterprise network for candidates to assess and attack;

		- appropriate vulnerable software (e.g., web applications, services, drivers);

		- user emulation software;

		- 18 monitors (minimum 24” flat screens);

		- nine keyboards/mouse;

		- nine portable USB drives (min 32 GB);

		- access to workstation with GPnet and/or Internet access; and

		- one network enabled colour ink jet printer.

6.	Consumable items:

	- CD / DVD ROMs (spindle);

	- 8.5 x 11 inch (Letter) printer paper;

	- whiteboard markers;

	- whiteboard erasers;

	- whiteboard cleaner (750 mL bottle); and

	- paper towel (roll/package).

7.	Audio-visual equipment: Projector and screen or large monitor (min 80 inches).

				**TRAINING LIMITATION**

				8.	Nil.

- **COURSE CAPACITY**

9.	The capacity per course is:

- optimum – 6;

- maximum load – 8; and

- minimum – 4.

			- Exceeding the maximum course capacity will require the following additional resources:

- instructors: (candidate/instructor ratio) 4:1;

- facilities: (candidate/classroom and lab ratio) 8:1; and

- equipment: increase in classroom/lab requirements by number of additional candidates IAW paragraph 5.

- The impact of not meeting the minimum course load is the serial will be rescheduled or cancelled.

Permission is required from the TA to deviate from the prescribed course capacity.

- **COURSE LOADING**

The authority to load members onto this course lies with the CYPER OP Career Manager, Requirements Advisor (RA) (D Cyber Ops FD/Comd CFIOG) and the TA.  

- **SCHEDULING**

Training will be scheduled by the Combat Training Centre (CTC) in MITE, in consultation with CFSCE and MPGTG HQ, to meet the training requirements.

- **PREREQUISITES**

To be selected for this course the loading authority must ensure that candidates have been awarded the CYBER OP Pte Rank Qualification (ALJQ).

- **QUALIFICATION**

Successful completion of this course constitutes the primary means of attaining the CYBER OP Red Analyst (ALRM) qualification. Additionally, the following MITE course ID 121947 will be granted.

- **ENVIRONMENTAL CONSIDERATIONS**

Nil.

- **LANGUAGE OF INSTRUCTION**

The language of instruction shall be in accordance with the stated tasking contained in the "Official Languages Act" (DAOD 5039-6).

- **EVALUATION**

Program evaluation shall be conducted IAW CFITES Volume 11 – Evaluation of Instructional Programmes.

- **PRIOR LEARNING ASSESSMENT AND RECOGNITION (PLAR)**

PLARs shall be conducted in accordance with DAOD 5031-1 – Canadian Forces Military Equivalencies Program and CFITES Volume 12 – Canadian Forces Military Equivalencies Programme: Prior Learning Assessment.

- **SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS / REMARKS**

Nil.

- **ASSESSMENT DETAILS**

- **GENERAL**

Performance checks (PC) and critical enabling checks (EC) are based on the Performance Objectives (PO) in Chapter 2, and the Enabling Objectives (EOs) in Chapter 4 of this QSP.  The standards identified in the POs must be met to achieve this qualification.

- **ENABLING CHECKS (ECs****)**

ECs are practical or written tests that help the candidate and the instructors recognize progress or gaps in learning, so that confirmation or corrective action (such as additional practice or remedial instruction) can be provided. They also reinforce learning, so that it will be better retained for longer periods. ECs are designed to give feedback to both the instructor(s) and candidates. Marks for non-critical ECs are **not** calculated in the candidate’s final mark.

- **CRITICAL EC****S**

For the purposes of this QSP, critical ECs are written or practical summative tests used to determine if the candidate has met the knowledge or skill-based requirements necessary to continue to the next step. Supplemental critical ECs are administered upon failure of a critical EC and must be administered in the same format as the original critical EC.

- **PERFORMANCE CHECKS (PCs)**

PCs are practical, summative tests used to determine if the candidate has met the standard under the conditions of the PO (where possible) and hence provide proof that the candidate can do the job. Supplemental PCs are administered upon failure of a PC and must be in the same format and have the same standard as the original PC.

- **RESPONSIBILITY FOR TESTING**

Assessment of candidates shall be conducted IAW CFITES Volume 7 – Evaluation of Learners.

Standards provide quality control by administering and scoring PCs. Any PC not administered or invigilated by Standards staff will be administered by a non-involved instructor designated by the senior instructor. ECs are the responsibility of the instructional staff.

- **PROGRESS MONITORING**

Monitoring learning is the responsibility of all instructional and Standards staff and instructional supervisors. Monitoring is done IAW CFITES Volume 6 – Conduct of Instruction Programmes and training establishment (TE) SOPs.

Monitoring is required in order to provide the following:

	- candidate interview and discussion of progress to date. A candidate who experiences difficulty is to be informed of the consequences of further failure(s) or marginal assessment and disposition options (e.g., retraining, return to unit [RTU]);

	- feedback on the effectiveness of the training design; and

- information for Progress Review Boards (PRBs). PRBs are conducted IAW Canadian Army Order (CAO) 24-8 IT&E Policies and Procedures Annex E, or the IT&E instruction currently in place. As a result of poor performance or conduct, a member may appear before a PRB. The PRB will make a recommendation to the Commandant regarding the disposition of the candidate. Recommended action will normally be one of the following:

- continue training after being formally warned to show improvement in accordance with DAOD 5019-4, Remedial Measures;

- retrain in accordance with PRB recommendations; or

- cease training.

Record file: A candidate's record file shall be maintained and reflect the following:

- completion of essential training activities required by POs;

		- results of ECs, critical ECs/written tests, and PCs as specified in the assessment plan at Annex E;

	- identification of elements requiring observations such as leadership, participation, qualities of character (e.g., non-commissioned member [NCM]-like or professional qualities) and/or other related assessment factors; and

- interviewing / counselling results.

Course Critiques: Sessions will be held to obtain feedback on learning activities, the presentation of training programme content and administration procedures. This does not preclude candidates from commenting on positive matters or reporting problems as they arise.

- **REMOVAL FROM TRAINING**

The Commandant may direct that a candidate be removed from training:

- when the candidate's progress is below the minimum standard and there 			is virtually no likelihood that the required standard will be attained; or

- when the candidate's continued presence is adversely affecting the 				training, safety or morale of the other candidates; or

- for administrative or disciplinary reasons.

When a candidate is removed from the course for any reason, the TE shall ensure that this information is recorded in MITE.

- **REPORTS**

Formal Course: A Canadian Forces Course Report, Form CF 377, shall be prepared and distributed in accordance with DAOD 5031-9. The course report shall be descriptive, identifying the candidate's strengths and weaknesses.

On-Job Performance Requirement: Nil.

Remarks: Nil.

