**MUST BE GRANTED BY ****COS ****MPGTG **

**FOR COMMANDER MPGTG**

# CHAPTER 4 – ENABLING OBJECTIVES

## PO 001 Conduct cyber reconnaissance

**EO 001.01**

- Performance: Conduct passive cyber reconnaissance.

- Conditions:

- given:

- target information (e.g., internet protocol [IP] range, domain name);

- analyst workstation with administrative/privileged access;

- attack framework software and tools;

- references; 

- other applicable tools (e.g., custom scripts, software plugins); and

- Internet access;

- denied: non-public information associated with the target (when applicable); and

- environment: classroom/computer lab equipped with Internet access.

- Standard: IAW refs B1, C15, C23 and C33–C35, the candidate shall conduct passive cyber reconnaissance by:

- adhering to laws and policies impacting Red Team operations, to include the Criminal Code of Canada and the National Defence Act;

- gathering target information using open source intelligence (OSINT) techniques (e.g., social media profiles, web sites), to include “Google hacking”;

- discovering additional target information with standard networking tools (e.g., whois, dig, tracert);

- gathering target information with web crawlers (e.g., Shodan);

- profiling organization’s access points (e.g., Wi-Fi, buildings); and

- generating wordlists based on organizational profile (e.g., CEWL, Crunch) to facilitate password guessing/attacks.

- Time Required:

- EO – 12 x 50 min periods;

- EC (EC#1) – 4 x 50 min periods (includes time for initial attempt, debriefing and additional practice if required, and retest if required); and

- PC 001 – 4 x 50 min periods (includes time for initial attempt, debriefing and additional practice if required, and retest if required).

- Instructional Methods:

- interactive lecture; and

- demonstration – performance.

- Training Aids:

- projector connected to instructor workstation;

- projector screen;

- whiteboard, with markers and eraser;

- PowerPoint slides containing key information related to lessons (e.g., definitions, diagrams); and

- Internet access.

- Test Details: See Annex E, Assessment Plan.

- References: B1, C15, C23, and C33–C35.

- Remarks: Nil.

- Teaching Points:

| Lesson Objective (LO) | Teaching Point | Method/ Activities | Time | Refs |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 001.01.01 Explain legal and policy considerations impacting Red Team operations. | Explain the: Difference between authorized and unauthorized hacking activities; Impact of legal and policy considerations on authorized Red Team operations; Potential for criminal proceedings under the Criminal Code (e.g., criminal charges under theft [e.g., stealing/copying software or data], mischief [e.g., destroying data, infecting a machine], unauthorized use of a computer); Potential for proceedings under the National Defence Act (e.g., negligent performance of duties [e.g., using untested malware, improper logging], offences in relation to documents [e.g., falsifying logs]); Need for SOPs and TTPs to prevent breaches of laws and policies during Red Team operations; and Difference between penetration testing and Red Team activities and the legal/policy implications. | Interactive Lecture | 2 x 50 mins | C33 C34 (Sections 183, 184, 322, 326, 327, 342.1, 342.2, 348, 402.1, 403 and 430) C35 (Sections 116, 117, 124, 125, 127, 129 and 130) |
| 001.01.02 Explain the purpose of passive reconnaissance. | Explain that: OSINT is used to profile an organisation’s online public presence; Google hacking is used to dig deeper into publicly available organizational information, including users, business units, software and hardware used, etc.; Standard networking tools are used to determine IP ranges, DNS registrations, email servers, etc.; Web crawlers are used to discover published content about the organisation’s web server(s), such as positional mailboxes; Access points are identified to determine possible entry points into the organization’s network environment (e.g., Wi-Fi, site maps, publicly accessible areas); Organizational positions are identified in order to assess possible targets for credential stealing or social engineering campaigns (e.g., spear phishing, whaling, road apples); and Word lists are built from found information to cue password guessing and cracking tools. | Interactive Lecture | 1 x 50 mins | B1 (pp. 5, 17 and 18), C15, and C23 |
| 001.01.03 Explain the importance of operational security (OPSEC) when conducting passive reconnaissance. | OPSEC considerations include: Using less attributable access (e.g., VPNs, proxy servers, third-party connectivity, privacy services) to mask identity during operations; Properly storing and handling collected information to protect client privacy and maintain security; and Applying OPSEC principles to maintain secrecy during operations (e.g., applying the “need-to-know” principle, securing desktops, using privacy screens). | Interactive Lecture | 1 x 50 mins | B1 (p. 4) |
| 001.01.04 Explain and demonstrate how to conduct passive reconnaissance. | Explain and demonstrate how to: Profile an organisation’s footprint with OSINT; Conduct passive cyber reconnaissance with full Google functionality; Conduct passive cyber reconnaissance with standard networking tools (e.g., whois, dig, tracert); Conduct passive cyber reconnaissance with web crawlers (e.g., Shodan); Profile an organization’s access points; Profile organizational positions; and Generate word lists (e.g., CEWL, Crunch). | Interactive Lecture and Demonstration | 4 x 50 mins | B1 (pp. 17 and 18), C15, and C23 |
| 001.01.05 Have candidates practice conducting passive cyber reconnaissance. | Have candidates conduct passive cyber reconnaissance as per LO 001.01.04, based on provided scenarios. | Performance (Lab) | 4 x 50 mins | B1 (pp. 4, 5, 17 and 18), C15, and C23 |

**EO 001.02**

- Performance: Conduct active cyber reconnaissance.

- Conditions:

- given:

- target information (e.g., IP address range, domain name);

- analyst workstation with administrative/privileged access;

- attack framework software and tools;

- references; 

- other applicable tools (e.g., custom scripts, software plugins); and

- Internet access;

- denied: non-public information associated with the target (when applicable); and

- environmental: classroom/computer lab equipped with internet access.

- Standard: IAW refs B1, C5, C22 and C33–C35, the candidate shall conduct active cyber reconnaissance by:

- adhering to laws and policies impacting Red Team operations, to include the Criminal Code of Canada and the National Defence Act;

- enumerating the target environment, to include:

- conducting targeted scanning;

- assessing running or available services;

- enumerating listening services;

- banner grabbing;

- enumerating defensive architecture; and

- enumerating local and network users;

- scanning for network vulnerabilities, to include:

- configuring vulnerability scan tool for targeted scanning; and

- interpreting vulnerability scanning tool results;

- assessing web applications for vulnerabilities; and

- scanning web applications for vulnerabilities.

- Time Required:

- EO – 15 x 50 min periods;

- EC (EC#2) – 4 x 50 min periods (includes time for initial attempt, debriefing and additional practice if required, and retest if required); and

- PC 001 – Time allocated at paragraph 4c of EO 001.01.

- Instructional Methods:

- interactive lecture; and

- demonstration – performance.

- Training Aids:

- projector connected to instructor workstation;

- projector screen;

- whiteboard, with markers and eraser; and

- PowerPoint slides containing key information related to lessons (e.g., definitions, diagrams).

- Test Details: See Annex E, Assessment Plan.

- Reference: B1, C5, C22 and C33–C35.

- Remarks: Legal and policy considerations impacting Red Team operations were addressed in lesson objective 001.01.01 of EO 001.01. They do not need to be retaught here; however, candidates should be reminded that those considerations also apply when conducting active cyber reconnaissance.

- Teaching Points:

| Lesson Objective (LO) | Teaching Point | Method/ Activities | Time | Refs |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 001.02.01 Explain the purpose of active cyber reconnaissance. | Explain that:  Active techniques are employed to conduct target environment enumeration, including that:  targeted scanning is used to determine specific accessible ports/services; listening services are enumerated to determine potential attack vectors; running or available services are assessed for vulnerabilities; banner grabbing is used to determine brand and/or version of listening service; defensive architecture is enumerated to determine bypass techniques needed; and local and network users are enumerated for brute force attacks and/or initial access; It is important to scan for network vulnerabilities, as interpretation of vulnerability scanning tool results allows the Red Analyst to identify existing vulnerabilities and assess the initial attack surface;  It is important to conduct web application vulnerability assessments in order to identify vulnerabilities and areas for improvement; and  Scanning web applications for vulnerabilities is conducted with tools such as nikto, dirbuster, and Burp Suite or Zap Proxy. | Interactive Lecture | 2 x 50 mins | C5, C22 (Ch 11.2.1) |
| 001.02.02 Explain OPSEC considerations when conducting active reconnaissance. | OPSEC considerations include: Using less attributable access (e.g., private networks [PNs], proxy servers, third-party connectivity, privacy services) to mask identity during operations; Properly storing and handling collected information in order to protect client privacy and maintain security; and Applying OPSEC principles to maintain secrecy during operations (e.g., applying the “need-to-know” principle, securing desktops, using privacy screens). | Interactive Lecture | 1 x 50 mins | B1 (p. 4) |
| 001.02.03 Explain and demonstrate how to conduct active cyber reconnaissance. | Explain and demonstrate how to: Enumerate the target environment: through targeted scanning; by assessing running or available services; by enumerating listening services; through banner grabbing; by enumerating defensive architecture; and by enumerating local and network users; Scan for network vulnerabilities by: configuring vulnerability scan tool for targeted scanning; and interpreting vulnerability scanning tool results; Assess web applications for vulnerabilities; and Scan web applications for vulnerabilities. | Interactive Lecture and Demonstration | 4 x 50 mins | C5 and C22 (Ch 11.2.1) |
| 001.02.04 Have candidates practice active cyber reconnaissance. | Candidates will practice conducting active cyber reconnaissance as per LO 001.02.03, based on provided scenarios. | Performance (Lab) | 8 x 50 mins | B1, C5 and C22 (Ch 11.2.1) |

**EO 001****.03**

- Performance: Assess extent of identified vulnerabilities.

- Conditions:

- given:

- target information (e.g., IP address range, domain name);

- analyst workstation with administrative/privileged access;

- attack framework software and tools;

- references; 

- other applicable tools (e.g., custom scripts, software plugins); and

- Internet access;

- denied: non-public information associated with the target (when applicable); and

- environment: classroom/computer lab equipped with internet access.

- Standard: IAW ref C16, the candidate shall assess the extent of identified vulnerabilities by:

- assessing availability of exploits; and

- searching databases for available exploits.

- Time Required:

- EO – 6 x 50 min periods;

- EC (EC#3) – 2 x 50 min periods (includes time for initial attempt, debriefing and additional practice if required, and retest if required); and

- PC 001 – time allocated at paragraph 4c of EO 001.01.

- Instructional Methods:

- interactive lecture; and

- demonstration – performance.

- Training Aids:

- projector connected to instructor workstation;

- projector screen;

- whiteboard, with markers and eraser; and

- PowerPoint slides containing key information related to lessons (e.g., definitions, diagrams).

- Test Details: See Annex E, Assessment Plan.

- Reference: C16.

- Remarks: Nil.

- Teaching Points:

| Lesson Objective (LO) | Teaching Point | Method/ Activities | Time | Refs |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 001.03.01 Explain why the Red Analyst must assess the extent of identified vulnerabilities. | Explain that: The availability of exploits is assessed to determine the most vulnerable attack surface; and Databases are searched for available exploits for service exploitation. Explain that for every possible attack surface there are usually multiple exploits to consider for use. The Red Analyst must consider what the desired effect is before selecting an available exploit for use (e.g., remote non-privileged code execution with high success probability versus remote privileged code execution with low success probability). Considerations that must be factored into exploit selection include, but are not limited to: detectability, complexity, reliability, repeatability, and effects on target stability. | Interactive Lecture | 1 x 50 mins | C16 |
| 001.03.02 Explain and demonstrate how to assess the extent of identified vulnerabilities. | Explain and demonstrate how to: Assess availability of exploits to determine most vulnerable attack surface; and Search databases for available exploits for service exploitation. | Interactive Lecture and Demonstration | 1 x 50 mins | C16 |
| 001.03.03 Have candidates practice assessing the extent of identified vulnerabilities. | As per LO 001.03.02, and given a provided scenario and network range with vulnerable services to scan, candidates will practice: Assessing availability of exploits to determine most vulnerable attack surface; and Searching databases for available exploits for service exploitation. | Performance (Lab) | 4 x 50 mins | C16 |

**EO 001****.04**

- Performance: Determine configuration changes required to conduct network evaluation.

- Conditions:

- given:

- target information (e.g., IP address range, domain name);

- analyst workstation with administrative/privileged access;

- attack framework software and tools;

- references; 

- other applicable tools (e.g., custom scripts, software plugins); and

- Internet access;

- denied: non-public information associated with the target (when applicable); and

- environment: classroom/computer lab equipped with Internet access.

- Standard: IAW refs C14, C22 (Chapter 5) and C26, and given target information, the candidate shall determine configuration changes required to conduct network evaluation by:

- determining current and desired state of the network; and

- configuring/reconfiguring Red Team infrastructure (e.g., IP configuration, DNS settings, proxy usage). 

- Time Required:

- EO – 4 x 50 min periods;

- EC – nil; and

- PC 001 – time allocated at paragraph 4c of EO 001.01.

- Instructional Methods:

- interactive lecture; and

- demonstration – performance.

- Training Aids:

- projector connected to instructor workstation;

- projector screen;

- whiteboard, with markers and eraser; and

- PowerPoint slides containing key information related to lessons (e.g., definitions, diagrams).

- Test Details: See Annex E, Assessment Plan.

- References: C14, C22 and C26.

- Remarks: Ref C26 (Red Team Infrastructure Wiki [GitHub]) may be outdated or not in use by the time this training is developed and delivered. In this case, the Red Team should be asked to push the new material for current threat modelling infrastructure techniques, and be consulted on occasion for any major changes.

- Teaching Points:

| Lesson Objective (LO) | Teaching Point | Method/ Activities | Time | Refs |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 001.04.01 Explain the importance of determining configuration changes required to conduct network evaluation. | Explain the importance of: Determining the current and desired state of the network in order to assess the changes required to conduct network evaluation; and Configuring/reconfiguring Red Team infrastructure in order to maintain functions of infrastructure such as C2 nodes (e.g., domain registration, IP allocation, C2 profile), firewall and port forwarding settings (e.g., iptables, ufw), proxy chaining, etc. Discuss the various changes that will need to be made to the host operation system (OS) of the attack framework(s), the configuration of the attack framework itself, and other elements used (e.g., beacons/ callbacks). | Interactive Lecture | 1 x 50 mins | C14,   C22 (Chs 5.1 and 5.3), and C26 |
| 001.04.02 Explain and demonstrate how to determine configuration changes required to conduct network evaluation. | Explain and demonstrate how to: Determine current state of the network using known or available network information (e.g., network diagrams/maps, bandwidth limitations, address resolution protocol [ARP] tables, dynamic host configuration protocol [DHCP] leases); Determine desired state of the network in order to conduct network evaluation under ideal conditions; and Configure/reconfigure Red Team infrastructure in order to maintain functions of infrastructure such as C2 nodes (e.g., domain registration, IP allocation, C2 profile), firewall and port forwarding settings (e.g., iptables, ufw), proxy chaining, etc. | Interactive Lecture and Demonstration | 1 x 50 mins | C14, C22 (Chs 5.1 and 5.3), and C26 |
| 001.04.03 Have candidates practice determining configuration changes required to conduct network evaluation. | Have candidates practice determining configuration changes required to conduct network evaluation as per LO 001.04.02, based on provided scenarios. | Performance (Lab) | 2 x 50 mins | C14, C22 (Chs 5.1 and 5.3) and C26 |

## PO 002 Gain access to a target device or network environment

**EO ****00****2.01**

- Performance: Attack remote services with attack tools and frameworks.

- Conditions:

- given:

					- analyst workstation;

					- cyber targeting package;

					- applicable references;

					- cyber range infrastructure; 

					- Windows and Linux strike package; and

					- internet access (Exploit-db.com, CVE Database);

					- denied: nil; and

- environment: classroom/computer lab equipped with internet access.

- Standard: IAW specified refs, the candidate shall attack remote services with attack tools and frameworks by:

- exploiting remote services IAW C17 (Chapters 5, 6, and 11–14), C20 (Chapters 4 and 5), C22 (Chapters 1–10) and C28 (Fundamentals), to include:

- prioritizing appropriate exploits;

- selecting appropriate exploits;

- configuring exploit functionality;

- generating payloads with an attack framework;

- setting up attack framework infrastructure to handle payload callbacks; 

- configuring attack software;

- delivering payloads with exploits; and

- interpreting the result of exploit delivery;

- attacking external authentication mechanisms IAW refs C5 (/wiki/Credential_Access), C17 (Chapters 2, 5 and 6), C22 (Chapters 1 –10) and C28 (Fundamentals), to include conducting:

- password guessing;

- brute force authentication attacks;

- dictionary attacks; and

- distributed authentication attack; and

- bypassing security access controls and active defences IAW refs C5 (/wiki/Defense_Evasion), C9 (Chapters 13 and 14), C12 (Chapters 9 and 14), C14 (Chapter 7) and C17 (Chapter 7), to include:

- selecting appropriate payloads to bypass firewalls and deep packet inspection; and

- obfuscating artifacts with basic obfuscation techniques and routines.

- Time Required:

- EO – 60 x 50 min periods;

- Critical EC (EC#4) – 8 x 50 min periods (includes time for initial attempt, debriefing and additional practice if required, and retest if required); and

- PC 002 – 8 x 50 min periods (includes time for initial attempt, debriefing and additional practice if required, and retest if required).

- Instructional Methods:

- interactive lecture; and

- demonstration – performance.

- Training Aids:

- projector connected to instructor workstation;

- projector screen;

- whiteboard, with markers and eraser; 

- cyber range infrastructure (Multiple Linux and Windows VMs, in a wide variety of network environments with an appropriate strike package);

- PowerPoint slides containing key information related to lessons (e.g., definitions, diagrams); and

- internet access.

- Test Details: See Annex E, Assessment Plan.

- References: C5, C9, C12, C14, C17, C20, C22 and C28.

- Remarks: 

- EO 002.02 will also be assessed in EC#4.

- EC#4 and PC 002 are each allotted eight 50-minute periods for scheduling purposes, but candidates are given a window of 16 continuous hours to complete the activities. 

- Teaching Points:

| Lesson Objective (LO) | Teaching Point | Method/ Activities | Time | Refs |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 002.01.01 Explain attack framework functionality. | Explain attack:  Frameworks; Framework modules; Framework auxiliary modules; Framework exploit modules; Framework payload modules; Framework post exploitation modules; and Framework handlers. | Interactive Lecture | 1 x 50 mins | C17 (Chs 2, 6, 8 and 9), C20 (Chs 1-5, 11, and 12), C22 (Chs 1-10) and C28 (Funda-mentals) |
| 002.01.02 Explain exploit selection and prioritization techniques. | Explain: Common vulnerabilities and exposures; Exploit databases; Exploit stability; Likelihood of exploit success; and Defensive architecture considerations in exploit selection. | Interactive Lecture | 1 x 50 mins | C9 (Chs 8-14), C16, C17 (Chs 4 and 5), C20 (Chs 4-7) and C28 (multi-sensory functional [MSF] integration) |
| 002.01.03 Explain payload selection. | Explain: Payload options; Payload handlers; Bind-connection payloads; Reserve-connection payloads; Beaconing payloads; Alternate C2 methods for payloads (e.g., Windows management instrumentation [WMI] name spaces, server message block [SMB] relays, filesystem covert channels); and Beacon relays. | Interactive Lecture | 1 x 50 mins | C17 (Chs 6 and 7), C20 (Chs 4-7) and C28 (Funda-mentals) |
| 002.01.04 Explain exploit configuration and delivery. | Explain: Exploit configuration options, selections, and best practices; Exploit validation; Exploit delivery; and Payload handler selection and configuration. | Interactive Lecture | 1 x 50 mins | C17 (Chs 6 and 7), C20 (Chs 4-7) and C28 (Funda-mentals) |
| 002.01.05 Explain how defensive architecture impacts exploit and payload selection. | Explain: Defensive architecture; How to bypass network and host firewall configurations; How to bypass deep packet inspection; How to bypass ingress/egress filtering; How to evade signature detection; and Manual payload and exploit obfuscation. | Interactive Lecture | 1 x 50 mins | C17 (Ch 7), C20 (Chs 6 and 7) and C28 (Funda-mentals) |
| 002.01.06 Explain and demonstrate basic obfuscation techniques. | Explain and demonstrate: Base64 encoding; Custom Base64 encoding techniques; Static XOR encoding; Dynamic XOR encoding; Replacement and ROTation (ROT) ciphers; and Stream ciphers. | Interactive Lecture and Demonstration | 4 x 50 mins | C3 (Chs 2-4 and 7-10), C27 (Ch 13) and C28 (Funda-mentals) |
| 002.01.07 Have candidates practice implementing basic obfuscation techniques. | As per LO 002.01.06 and based on provided scenarios, candidates will practice implementing: Base64 encoding; Custom Base64 encoding techniques; Static XOR encoding; Dynamic XOR encoding; Replacement and ROT ciphers; and Stream ciphers. | Performance (Lab) | 12 x 50 mins | C3 (Chs 2-4 and 7-10), C27 (Ch 13) and C28 (Funda-mentals) |
| 002.01.08 Demonstrate gaining access by exploiting remote services. | Demonstrate: Vulnerability discovery; Exploit selection; Exploit configuration; Payload selection and configuration; Configuring handlers; Delivering exploits; and Defensive architecture evasion and bypass. | Demonstration | 8 x 50 mins | C8, C17 (Chs 1-11 and 17), C20 (Chs 1-11) and C28 (Funda-mentals) |
| 002.01.09 Have candidates practice gaining initial access by exploiting remote services. | Based on provided scenarios, candidates will practice: Discovering exploitable services; Selecting appropriate exploits; Testing appropriate exploits; Configuring exploits; Selecting appropriate payloads; Configuring appropriate payloads; Configuring payload handlers; Exploiting remote services; and Bypassing defensive architecture. | Performance (Lab) | 24 x 50 mins | C8, C17 (Chs 1-11 and 17), C20 (Chs 1-11) and C28 (Funda-mentals) |
| 002.01.10 Explain authentication service attacks. | Explain: Authentication service discovery; Dictionary attacks; Brute force attacks; Distributed dictionary attacks; and How to aggregate, generate, and mutate wordlists. | Interactive Lecture | 2 x 50 mins | C5 (/wiki/Credential_Access), C17 (Chs 2, 5 and 6), C22 (Chs 1-10) and C28 (Funda-mentals) |
| 002.01.11 Demonstrate authentication service attacks. | Demonstrate the authentication service attacks outlined in LO 002.01.10. | Demonstration | 1 x 50 mins | C5 (/wiki/Credential_Access), C17 (Chs 2, 5 and 6), C22 (Chs 1-10) and C28 (Funda-mentals) |
| 002.01.12 Have candidates practice authentication service attacks. | Candidates will practice conducting the authentication service attacks outlined in LO 002.01.10, based on provided scenarios. | Performance (Lab) | 4 x 50 mins | C5 (/wiki/Credential_Access), C17 (Chs 2, 5 and 6), C22 (Chs 1-10) and C28 (Funda-mentals) |

**EO ****00****2.02**

- Performance: Expand network access with an attack framework and tools.

- Conditions:

	- given:

					- analyst workstation;

					- cyber targeting package;

					- applicable references;

					- cyber range infrastructure; 

					- Windows and Linux strike package; and

					- Internet access (Exploit-db.com, CVE database);

	- denied: nil; and

	- environment: classroom/computer lab equipped with internet access.

- Standard: IAW specified refs, the candidate shall expand network access with attack framework and tools by:

	- attacking domain authentication mechanisms IAW refs C5 (/wiki/Credential_Access, and /wiki/lateral_movement), C14 (Chapters 4 and 7), C18, C19, C20 (Chapter 8), and C29 (Chapter 10), to include conducting:

	- pass-the-hash attacks;

	- token forgery;

	- over-pass-the-hash attacks;

	- pass-the-ticket attacks;

	- golden ticket attacks;

	- silver ticket attacks; and

	- kerberoasting;

- escalating host privileges IAW refs C5 (/wiki/Privilege_Escalation), C12, C14 (Chapter 4), C17 (Chapter 7), C20 (Chapter 8.15) and C28 (Privilege Escalation), to include:

- exploiting device drivers;

- exploiting local services;

- exploiting software running at higher privilege level;

- exploiting administrative scripts;

- exploiting filesystem misconfigurations;

- exploiting common software deficiencies (e.g., relative file paths, calling programs in world writable directories, improperly secured config files); and

- exploiting common system misconfigurations;

- abusing domain trust relationships IAW refs C18 and C19;

- abusing domain misconfiguration errors IAW refs C18 and C19; and

- exploiting internal hosts, devices, and services IAW refs C5 (/wiki/Credential_Access, /wiki/lateral_movement, and /wiki/Priviledge_Escalation), C12, C14 (Chapter 4), C17 (Chapter 7) and C20 (Chapters 6 and 8).

- Time Required:

- EO – 47 x 50 min periods;

- EC#4 (critical EC) – time allocated at paragraph 4b of EO 002.01; and

- PC 002 – time allocated at paragraph 4c of EO 002.01.

- Instructional Methods:

- interactive lecture; and

- demonstration – performance.

- Training Aids:

- projector connected to instructor workstation;

- projector screen;

- whiteboard, with markers and eraser; 

- cyber range infrastructure (Multiple Linux and Windows VMs, in a wide variety of network environments with an appropriate strike package);

- PowerPoint slides containing key information related to lessons (e.g., definitions, diagrams); and

- Internet access.

- Test Details: See Annex E, Assessment Plan.

- References: C5, C12, C14, C17–C20, C28 and C29.

- Remarks: Nil.

- Teaching Points:

| Lesson Objective (LO) | Teaching Point | Method/ Activities | Time | Refs |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 002.02.01 Explain Kerberos authentication. | Explain: Kerberos functional description; Kerberos key terms (i.e., ticket-granting ticket [TGT], ticket-granting service [TGS], Session key, Windows NT LAN Manager [NTLM]); TGT request process; TGS request process; and Kerberos weaknesses. | Interactive Lecture | 1 x 50 mins | C18, C19 and C29 (Ch 10) |
| 002.02.02 Explain and demonstrate domain authentication attacks. | Explain and demonstrate the following domain authentication attacks: Pass-the-hash attacks; Token forgery; Over-pass-the-hash attacks; Pass-the-ticket attacks; Golden ticket attacks; Silver ticket attacks; and Kerberoasting. Describe defensive mitigations and countermeasures. | Interactive Lecture and Demonstration | 4 x 50 mins | C18 and C19 |
| 002.02.03 Have the candidates practice domain authentication attacks. | Based on provided scenarios, the candidates will practice carrying out the attacks listed in LO 002.02.02. | Performance (Lab) | 8 x 50 mins | C18 and C19 |
| 002.02.04 Explain and demonstrate privilege escalation. | Explain and demonstrate: Access token manipulation; Abusing accessibility features; AppCert Dynamic Linked Library (DLL) abuse; AppInit DLL abuse; Application shimming; Bypassing User Access Control (UAC); DLL search order hijacking; Exploiting install software and services; Extra Window memory injection; File system permission weakness; Process hooking; Image file execution option injection; Path interception; Port monitor abuse; Process injection; Security Identifier (SID) history injection; Setuid and setgid abuse; Scheduled task abuse; Service registry permission weakness abuse; and Credential stealing. | Interactive Lecture and Demonstration | 8 x 50 mins | C1 (Ch 10), C5 (/wiki/Privilege_Escalation), C20 (Ch 8) and C28 (Privilege Escalation) |
| 002.02.05 Have candidates practice privilege escalation. | Based on provided scenarios, the candidates will practice privilege escalation techniques listed in LO 002.02.04. | Performance (Lab) | 24 x 50 mins | C1 (Ch 10), C5 (/wiki/Privilege_Escalation), C20 (Ch 8) and C28 (Privilege Escalation) |
| 002.02.06 Explain and demonstrate domain trust abuse. | Review domain trusts; Review domain trust flaws; Review domain trust enumeration; and Demonstrate conducting domain trust abuse. | Interactive Lecture and Demonstration | 1 x 50 mins | C18 and C19 |
| 002.02.07 Have candidates practice domain trust abuse. | Candidates will practice domain trust enumeration and abuse as per LO 002.02.06, based on provided scenarios. | Performance (Lab) | 1 x 50 mins | C18 and C19 |

**EO ****00****2.03**

- Performance: Modify existing tool and exploit source code.

- Conditions:

	- given:

					- analyst workstation;

					- cyber targeting package;

					- applicable references;

					- cyber range infrastructure; 

					- Windows and Linux strike package; and

					- internet access (Exploit-db.com, CVE Database);

	- denied: nil; and

	- environment: classroom/computer lab equipped with internet access.

- Standard: IAW refs C1, C2, C12, C17 (Chapters 13–16) and C31 (Chapters 1–3), candidates shall modify existing tool and exploit source code by:

	- analyzing modern binary exploitation techniques;

	- analyzing modern operating system (OS) and binary protection mechanisms;

	- analyzing tools source code;

	- modifying tool source code;

	- modifying and replacing exploit source code, to include:

	- adjusting address offsets;

	- replacing exploit payloads; and

	- adjusting exploit configuration;

	- maintaining and documenting modified tool and exploit;

	- maintaining and documenting cyber Red Team toolkit; and

	- testing modified tools and exploit.

- Time Required:

	- EO – 40 x 50 min periods;

	- EC – Nil; and

	- PC 002 – time allocated at paragraph 4c of EO 002.01.

- Instructional Methods:

	- interactive lecture; and

	- demonstration – performance.

- Training Aids:

- projector connected to instructor workstation;

- projector screen;

- whiteboard, with markers and eraser; 

- cyber range infrastructure (Multiple Linux and Windows VMs, in a wide variety of network environments with an appropriate strike package);

- PowerPoint slides containing key information related to lessons (e.g., definitions, diagrams); and

- Internet access.

- Test Details: See Annex E, Assessment Plan.

- References: C1, C2, C12, C17 and C31.

- Remarks: This EO focuses on making minor adjustments to source code for tools and exploits in high-level programming languages; as such, instruction should not exceed this.

- Teaching Points:

| Lesson Objective (LO) | Teaching Point | Method/ Activities | Time | Refs |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 002.03.01 Explain and demonstrate stack smashing with buffer overflows. | Explain and demonstrate: von Neumann Architecture; Stack frames; Extended Instruction Pointer (EIP); Stack buffers; and Stack buffer overflow exploitation. | Interactive Lecture and Demonstration | 8 x 50 mins | C12 (Chs 1-3), C17 (Chs 13-16) and C31 (Chs 1-3) |
| 002.03.02 Explain and demonstrate heap smashing with buffer overflows. | Explain and demonstrate: Malloc() and free() functionality; Heap structures; Heap overflows; and Heap overflow exploitation. | Interactive Lecture and Demonstration | 4 x 50 mins | C12 (Ch 5) |
| 002.03.03 Explain and demonstrate how to bypass binary and operating system protection mechanisms. | Explain and demonstrate: The NX (No Execute) bit; Data Execution Prevention (DEP); Stack canaries; Address Space Layout Randomization (ASLR); ASLR bypass techniques; ret-2-libc attacks; Return oriented programming (ROP); ROP gadgets; ROP chains; and Blind ROP. | Interactive Lecture and Demonstration | 6 x 50 mins | C12 (Ch 14) |
| 002.03.04 Explain and demonstrate how to modify exploit Proof of Concept (PoC) code. | Explain and demonstrate how to: Find PoC code; Read PoC code; Make modifications to PoC code in order to fit your requirements; and Use a debugger to debug PoC code. | Interactive Lecture and Demonstration | 2 x 50 mins | C12 (Chs 1-3 and 14), C17 (Chs 13-16) and C31 (Chs 1-3) |
| 002.03.05 Explain and demonstrate how to read and modify Red Team tool source code. | Explain and demonstrate: Tool development methodology; Structure of Red Team tools; Tool modification techniques; and How to test modified tools. | Interactive Lecture and Demonstration | 2 x 50 mins | C1, C12 (Chs 1-3 and 14), C17 (Chs 13-16) and C31 (Chs 1-3) |
| 002.03.06 Explain and demonstrate how to find, generate, and make basic modifications to shellcode. | Explain: Shell code; MSFvenom; How to find shellcode in online databases; and How to make basic modification of shellcode. Demonstrate how to find, generate, and make basic modifications to shellcode. | Interactive Lecture and Demonstration | 2 x 50 mins | C12 (Chs 3, 7, and 12), C17 (Chs 13-16) and C31 (Ch 5) |
| 002.03.07 Have candidates practice modifying exploits and Red Team tools. | In accordance with LOs 002.03.01-.06, candidates will practice modifying and testing tools and exploit source code based on provided scenarios. | Performance (Lab) | 16 x 50 mins | C1, C12 (Chs 1-3 and 14), C17 (Chs 13-16) and C31 (Chs 1-3 and 5) |

**EO ****00****2.04**

- Performance: Exploit web applications.

- Conditions:

- given:

					- analyst workstation;

					- cyber targeting package;

					- applicable references;

					- cyber range infrastructure; 

					- Windows and Linux strike package; and

					- internet access (Exploit-db.com, CVE Database);

- denied: nil; and

- environment: classroom/computer lab equipped with internet access.

- Standard: IAW ref C11, the candidate shall exploit web applications with web exploitation tools (e.g., Burp Suite, SQLmap, SQL ninja, BEeF) by:

- assessing web applications for vulnerabilities, to include:

- scanning web applications with vulnerability assessment tools (e.g., ZAP, Burp suite, nikto); and

- manually searching web applications for common vulnerabilities (e.g., SQLi, XSS, command injection); and

- exploiting web applications with exploitation frameworks and tools, to include:

- exploiting Remote/Local File Inclusion (RFI/LFI);

- exploiting Structured Query Language (SQL) injection;

- exploiting cross site scripting (XSS);

- exploiting cross site request forgery (CSRF);

- exploiting command injection;

- exploiting web authentication flaws; and

- installing web based backdoors, to include:

- selecting appropriate web shells;

- uploading and installing web shells;

- modifying web shells; and

- obfuscating web shells.

- Time Required:

- EO – 42 x 50 min periods;

- EC (EC#5) – 8 x 50 min periods (includes time for initial attempt, debriefing and additional practice if required, and retest if required); and

- PC 002 – time allocated at paragraph 4c of EO 002.01.

- Instructional Methods:

- interactive lecture; and

- demonstration – performance.

- Training Aids:

- projector connected to instructor workstation;

- projector screen;

- whiteboard, with markers and eraser; 

- cyber range infrastructure (Multiple Linux and Windows VMs, in a wide variety of network environments with an appropriate strike package);

- PowerPoint slides containing key information related to lessons (e.g., definitions, diagrams); and

- Internet access.

- Test Details: See Annex E, Assessment Plan.

- Reference: C11.

- Remarks: Nil.

- Teaching Points:

| Lesson Objective (LO) | Teaching Point | Method/ Activities | Time | Refs |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 002.04.01 Explain and demonstrate web vulnerability scanning. | Explain and demonstrate: Vulnerability scanners; and Result interpretation. | Interactive Lecture and Demonstration | 1 x 50 mins | C11 (Chs 1-4) |
| 002.04.02 Explain and demonstrate file inclusion vulnerabilities. | Explain and demonstrate: RFI; LFI; Why file inclusion vulnerabilities exist; and How to exploit file inclusion vulnerabilities. | Interactive Lecture and Demonstration | 1 x 50 mins | C11 (Chs 13 and 21) |
| 002.04.03 Have candidates practice exploiting file inclusion vulnerabilities. | Based on provided scenarios, candidates will practice exploiting file inclusion vulnerabilities as per LO 002.04.02. | Performance (Lab) | 4 x 50 mins | C11 (Chs 13 and 21) |
| 002.04.04 Explain and demonstrate SQL injection. | Explain and demonstrate: SQL; Why injection flaws exist; How to exploit SQL injection; and Blind SQL injection. | Interactive Lecture and Demonstration | 4 x 50 mins | C11 (Ch 9) |
| 002.04.05 Have candidates practice SQL injection. | Based on provided scenarios, candidates will practice SQL injection as per LO 002.04.04. | Performance (Lab) | 4 x 50 mins | C11 (Ch 9) |
| 002.04.06 Explain and demonstrate XSS. | Explain and demonstrate: Javascript; Why XSS flaws exist; and How to exploit XSS flaws. | Interactive Lecture and Demonstration | 4 x 50 mins | C11 (Ch 12) |
| 002.04.07 Have candidates practice XSS. | Based on provided scenarios, candidates will practice XSS as per LO 002.04.06. | Performance (Lab) | 4 x 50 mins | C11 (Ch 12) |
| 002.04.08 Explain and demonstrate CSRF. | Explain and demonstrate: Web session management; Why CSRF flaws exist; and How to exploit CSRF flaws. | Interactive Lecture and Demonstration | 1 x 50 mins | C11 (Ch 13) |
| 002.04.09 Have candidates practice CSRF. | Based on provided scenarios, candidates will practice CSRF as per LO 002.04.08. | Performance (Lab) | 1 x 50 mins | C11 (Ch 13) |
| 002.04.10 Explain and demonstrate command injection. | Explain and demonstrate: Web command execution; Why command injection flaws exist; and How to exploit CSRF flaws. | Interactive Lecture and Demonstration | 1 x 50 mins | C11 (Ch 13) |
| 002.04.11 Have candidates practice command injection. | Based on provided scenarios, candidates will practice command injection as per LO 002.04.10. | Performance (Lab) | 4 x 50 mins | C11 (Ch 13) |
| 002.04.12 Explain and demonstrate authentication flaws. | Explain and demonstrate: Web authentication; Why web authentication flaws exist; and How to exploit web authentication flaws. | Interactive Lecture and Demonstration | 1 x 50 mins | C11 (Chs 5-8) |
| 002.04.13 Have candidates practice exploiting authentication flaws. | Based on provided scenarios, candidates will practice exploiting authentication flaws as per LO 002.04.12. | Performance (Lab) | 4 x 50 mins | C11 (Chs 5-8) |
| 002.04.14 Explain and demonstrate web shell modification. | Explain and demonstrate: Web shells; Web shell C2; Web shell obfuscation; and How to generate and modify web shells. | Interactive Lecture and Demonstration | 2 x 50 mins | C11 (Ch 21) |
| 002.04.15 Have candidates practice web shell usage and modification. | Based on provided scenarios, candidates will practice web shell usage and modification as per LO 002.04.14. | Performance (Lab) | 6 x 50 mins | C11 (Ch 21) |

**EO ****00****2.05**

- Performance: Support social engineering operations.

- Conditions:

- given:

					- analyst workstation;

					- cyber targeting package;

					- applicable references;

					- cyber range infrastructure; 

					- Windows and Linux strike package; and

					- internet access (Exploit-db.com, CVE Database);

- denied: nil; and

- environment: classroom/computer lab equipped with internet access.

- Standard: IAW refs C5, C9 (Chapters 14–18), C10, C14 (Chapters 4 and 5), C17 (Ch 10) and C20, candidates shall support social engineering operations by:

- configuring social engineering attack infrastructure;

- generating social engineering payloads with an attack framework;

- building social engineering payloads;

- generating social engineering payload deliver mechanisms with an attack framework;

- building social engineering payload delivery mechanisms;

- testing payloads and delivery mechanisms in a realistic environment; and

- exploiting workstations with client side attacks, to include:

- conducting drive-by-downloads;

- delivering signed java applets;

- exploiting web browser vulnerabilities;

- orchestrating watering hole attacks; and

- poisoning removable digital media and peripheral devices.

- Time Required:

- EO – 14 x 50 min periods;

- EC – nil; and

- PC 002 – time allocated at paragraph 4c of EO 002.01.

- Instructional Methods:

- interactive lecture; and

- demonstration – performance.

- Training Aids:

- projector connected to instructor workstation;

- projector screen;

- whiteboard, with markers and eraser; 

- cyber range infrastructure (Multiple Linux and Windows VMs, in a wide variety of network environments with an appropriate strike package);

- PowerPoint slides containing key information related to lessons (e.g., definitions, diagrams); and

- internet access.

- Test Details: See Annex E, Assessment Plan.

- References: C5, C9, C10, C14, C17 and C20.

- Remarks: The scope of this EO is to be further defined by mandate. At this stage, candidates will only be trained to use and deploy social engineering infrastructure and tools.

- Teaching Points:

| Lesson Objective (LO) | Teaching Point | Method/ Activities | Time | Refs |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 002.05.01 Explain social engineering operations. | Define social engineering. Explain methods and methodology. Present some social engineering case studies. Explain when and why social engineering is effective. | Interactive Lecture | 2 x 50 mins | C5 (/wiki/initial_access), C10 (Chs 1-3), C14 (Ch 2) and C20 (Ch 7) |
| 002.05.02 Explain and demonstrate social engineering attack vectors. | Explain and demonstrate: Conducting drive-by-downloads; Delivering signed java applets; Exploiting web browser vulnerabilities; Orchestrating watering hole attacks; and Poisoning removable digital media and peripheral devices. | Interactive Lecture and Demonstration | 2 x 50 mins | C5 (/wiki/initial_access), C9 (Ch 18), C10 (Chs 1-3), C14 (Ch 2) and C20 (Ch 7) |
| 002.05.03 Explain and demonstrate social engineering tools and attack infrastructure. | Describe social engineering attack tools and frameworks. Explain and demonstrate configuring attack tools and frameworks for a social engineering operation. Explain and demonstrate developing payloads to integrate with attack frameworks. | Interactive Lecture and Demonstration | 2 x 50 mins | C5 (/wiki/initial_access), C9 (Ch 18), C10 (Chs 1-3), C14 (Ch 2) and C20 (Ch 7) |
| 002.05.04 Have candidates practice supporting social engineering attacks. | As per LO 002.05.02 and based on provided scenarios, candidates will practice: Conducting drive-by-downloads; Delivering signed java applets; Exploiting web browser vulnerabilities; Orchestrating watering hole attacks; and Poisoning removable digital media and peripheral devices. | Performance (Lab) | 8 x 50 mins | C5 (/wiki/initial_access), C9 (Ch 18), C10 (Chs 1-3), C14 (Ch 2) and C20 (Ch 7) |

## PO 003 Generate authorized adverse effects on target network devices

**EO 003.01**

- Performance: Establish a foothold on a target nework through use of an attack framework.

- Conditions:

- given:

- analyst workstation;

- analysis software and tools;

- references;

- suitable work area;

- internet access; and

- target information;

- denied: nil; and

- environment: classroom/computer lab equipped with internet access.

- Standard: IAW refs C1 (Chapter 8), C2 (Chapter 7), C5 (Chapters 4 and 10), C17 (Chapter 7), C18, C20 (Chapter 6), C24 (Chapter 6) and C25 (Chapter 13), the candidate shall establish a foothold on a target nework through use of an attack framework by:

- selecting an appropriate process for migration;

- migrating;

- establishing persistence with an attack framework;

- evading host based defences with an attack framework; 

- expanding network access by conducting lateral movement, to include:

- pivoting;

- port forwarding;

- forging security access tokens;

- analysing internal network for compromise potential;

- facilitating movement without use of malware utilising native administrative tools;

- tainting shared content;

- abusing misconfigured administration file shares; and

- implementing replication through removable media; and

- denying forensic artifacts to digital forensics and incident response (DFIR), to include covering tracks using an attack framework.

- Time Required:

- EO – 70 x 50 min periods;

- EC (EC#6) – 2 x 50 min periods (includes time for initial attempt, debriefing and additional practice if required, and retest if required); and

- PC 003 – 8 x 50 min periods (includes time for initial attempt, debriefing and additional practice if required, and retest if required).

- Instructional Methods:

- interactive lecture; and

- demonstration – performance.

- Training Aids:

- projector connected to instructor workstation;

- projector screen;

- whiteboard, with markers and eraser; and

- PowerPoint slides containing key information related to lessons (e.g., definitions, diagrams).

- Test Details: See Annex E, Assessment Plan.

- References: C1, C2, C5, C17, C18, C20, C24 and C25.

- Remarks: Nil.

- Teaching Points:

| Lesson Objective | Teaching Point | Method/ Activities | Time | Refs |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 003.01.01 Explain process migration and mechanisms. | Explain: Windows operating systems security controls; Linux operating systems security controls; and Process migration mechanisms. | Interactive Lecture | 2 x 50 mins | C5 (Ch 6), C18 and C24 (Ch 13) |
| 003.01.02 Explain and demonstrate how to select and migrate processes. | Explain and demonstrate how to select and migrate appropriate processes, to include: Identifying appropriate privilege level; and Identifying correct architecture. | Interactive Lecture and Demonstration | 2 x 50 mins | C5 (Ch 6), C18 and C24 (Ch 13) |
| 003.01.03 Have candidates practice migrating processes. | Candidates will practice selecting an appropriate process and migrating through an attack framework as per LO 003.01.02, based on provided scenarios. | Performance (Lab) | 2 x 50 mins | C5 (Ch 6), C18 and C24 (Ch 13) |
| 003.01.04 Explain technical considerations associated with establishing persistence. | Explain Windows and Linux: Boot processes; and Persistence mechanisms. | Interactive Lecture | 4 x 50 mins | C5 (Ch 4) and C25 (Ch 13) |
| 003.01.05 Explain and demonstrate how to establish persistence with a framework. | Explain and demonstrate how to establish persistence through an attack framework, to include:  Framework auto-persist;  Installing services;  Startup folders;  Logon scripts; and  Group Policy Objects (GPOs). f | Interactive Lecture and Demonstration | 4 x 50 mins | C5 (Ch 4) and C17 |
| 003.01.06 Have candidates practice establishing persistence. | Candidates will practice establishing persistence using an attack framework as per LO 003.01.05, based on provided scenarios. | Performance (Lab) | 16 x 50 mins | C5 (Ch 4) and C17 |
| 003.01.07 Explain evasion of host based defences. | Explain: Host based defences; and Evasion techniques. | Interactive Lecture | 2 x 50 mins | C5 (Ch 8, C17 (Ch 7), C20 and C24 (Ch 6) |
| 003.01.08 Explain and demonstrate how to evade host based defences. | Explain and demonstrate how to evade host based defences using an attack framework, to include: Bypassing User Account Controls (UACs); Disabling security tools; Validating account use; and Processing hollowing/injection. | Interactive Lecture and Demonstration | 2 x 50 mins | C1 (Ch 8), C2 (Ch 7), C5 (Ch 8), C17 (Ch 7), C20 and C24 (Ch 6) |
| 003.01.09 Have candidates practices evading host based defences. | Candidates will practice evading host based defences using an attack framework as per LO 003.01.08, based on provided scenarios. | Performance (Lab) | 4 x 50 mins | C5 (Ch 8), C17 (Ch 7), C20 and C24 (Ch 6) |
| 003.01.10 Explain network defence architecture’s impact on Red Team operations. | Explain network defence architecture’s impact on Red Team operations, to include: Kerberos authentication process; Domain trusts; Shares; Firewalls; and Intrusion Detection System (IDS)/Intrusion Prevention System (IPS). | Interactive Lecture | 2 x 50 mins | C24 (Ch 6) |
| 003.01.11 Explain lateral movement techniques and considerations. | Explain: Forging security access tokens; Analyzing internal network for compromise potential; Facilitating movement by utilizing native tools; Tainting shared content; Abusing misconfigured administrative file shares; Implementing replication through removeable media; Port forwarding; and Pivoting. | Interactive Lecture | 2 x 50 mins | C5 (Ch 10), C17 and C20 |
| 003.01.12 Explain and demonstrate conducting lateral movement with an attack framework. | Explain and demonstrate lateral movement techniques listed in LO 003.01.11. | Interactive Lecture and Demonstration | 4 x 50 mins | C5 (Ch 10), C17 and C20 |
| 003.01.13 Have candidates practice conducting lateral movement with an attack framework. | Candidates will practice conducting lateral movement using an attack framework as per LO 003.01.12, based on provided scenarios. | Performance (Lab) | 8 x 50 mins | C5 (Ch 10), C17 and C20 |
| 003.01.14 Explain forensics techniques and how to minimize a forensic footprint. | Explain:  DFIR techniques and procedures;  Various attack tools’ host and network footprints; and  Counter- and anti-DFIR techniques. | Interactive Lecture | 2 x 50 mins | C5 (Ch 10) |
| 003.01.15 Explain and demonstrate anti-DFIR techniques (covering tracks). | Explain and demonstrate covering tracks using an attack framework, to include: Log manipulation; Artifact destruction; Manipulating active defence behavior; Data hiding; Patching memory constructs; Import address table (IAT) hijacking; and Attacking forensic tools. | Interactive Lecture and Demonstration | 2 x 50 mins | C5 (Ch 10) |
| 003.01.16 Have candidates practices anti-DFIR techniques (covering tracks). | Candidates will practice covering tracks using an attack framework as per LO 003.01.15, based on a provided scenario. | Performance (Lab) | 12 x 50 mins | C5 (Ch 10) |

**EO 003.02**

- Performance: Maintain network access through use of an attack framework.

- Conditions:

- given:

- analyst workstation;

- analysis software and tools;

- references;

- suitable work area; and

- internet access;

- denied: nil; and

- environment: classroom/computer lab equipped with internet access.

- Standard: IAW refs C14, C17 and C20 (Chapters 1, 4, 5, 8 and 12), the candidate shall maintain network access through use of an attack framework by:

- maintaining redirector configurations;

- maintaining C2 domains;

- maintaining C2 beacons; and

- modifying malware behaviour, to include:

- attack framework malware functionality;

- attack framework malware profiles;

- beacon functionality; and

- beacon profiles.

- Time Required:

- EO – 35 x 50 min periods;

- EC  – nil; and

- PC 003 – time allocated at paragraph 4c of EO 003.01.

- Instructional Methods:

- interactive lecture; and

- demonstration – performance.

- Training Aids:

- projector connected to instructor workstation;

- projector screen;

- whiteboard, with markers and eraser; 

- PowerPoint slides containing key information related to lessons (e.g., definitions, diagrams);

- internet access; and

- attack framework software.

- Test Details: See Annex E, Assessment Plan.

- References: C14, C17 and C20.

- Remarks: Candidates will be given access to relevant attack framework software and be expected to understand the theory of the framework tool functionality before use. References for lesson objective 003.02.12 may be subject to change depending on the relevant attack framework being employed.

- Teaching Points:

| Lesson Objective (LO) | Teaching Point | Method/ Activities | Time | Refs |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 003.02.01 Explain Red Team C2. | Explain: Encrypted C2 channels; and Protocol tunneling, to include: Hyper text transfer protocol (http); DNS; and Server message block (smb). | Interactive Lecture | 4 x 50 mins | C14 (Ch 1) |
| 003.02.02 Explain the purpose of redirectors, types and implementation. | Explain: Purpose of redirectors; Types of redirectors, to include: Long term access; Short term access; Phishing; File hosting; and DNS; and Redirector implementation. | Interactive Lecture | 1 x 50 mins | C20 (Ch 4) |
| 003.02.03 Explain overall redirector and domain maintenance. | Explain: Procurement and implications of IP address space; Procurement and implication of domain names; Application of domain names; and Remedial actions on discovery. | Interactive Lecture | 1 x 50 mins | C20 (Ch 4.3 – 4.7) |
| 003.02.04 Demonstrate redirector and domain implementation. | Demonstrate: Deploying redirectors and domains; and Use of redirectors and domains. | Demonstration | 1 x 50 mins | C20 (Ch 4.3 – 4.7) |
| 003.02.05 Have candidates practice redirector and domain implementation. | As per LO 003.02.04 and based on provided scenarios, candidates will practice: Deploying redirectors and domains; and Using redirectors and domains. | Performance (Lab) | 2 x 50 mins | C20 (Ch 4.3 – 4.7) |
| 003.02.06 Explain attack framework and behaviour. | Define an attack framework.   Explain relevant attack frameworks (e.g., Metasploit, Cobalt Strike, Empire).   Explain relevant attack framework behaviours. | Interactive Lecture | 1 x 50 mins | C17 and C20 |
| 003.02.07 Explain and demonstrate modifying attack framework behaviour and functionality. | Explain and demonstrate how to modify attack framework functionality by creating related attack framework scripts (i.e., Cobalt Strike aggressor scripts). | Interactive Lecture and Demonstration | 4 x 50 mins | C20 (Ch 1.6) |
| 003.02.08 Have candidates practice modifying attack framework behaviour and functionality. | Candidates will practice modifying attack framework functionality (i.e., aggressor scripts in cobalt strike) as per LO 003.02.07, based on provided scenarios. | Performance (Lab) | 8 x 50 mins | C20 (Ch 1.6) |
| 003.02.09 Explain attack framework malware profiles. | Explain relevant attack framework malware profiles, to include: Purpose of modification; and Modifiable attributes. | Interactive Lecture | 1 x 50 mins | C20 (Ch 12) |
| 003.02.10 Explain and demonstrate modifying attack framework malware profiles. | Explain and demonstrate modifying relevant attack framework malware profiles (i.e., modulate HTTP cookie header and call back address in Cobalt Strike). | Interactive Lecture and Demonstration | 1 x 50 mins | C20 (Ch 12) |
| 003.02.11 Have candidates practice modifying attack framework malware profiles. | Candidates will practice modifying attack framework malware profiles as per LO 003.02.10, based on provided scenarios. | Performance (Lab) | 2 x 50 mins | C20 (Ch 12) |
| 003.02.12 Explain beacon functionality and types. | Explain:  Beacon functionality; and  Types of beacons, to include: http; smb; and dns. | Interactive Lecture | 1 x 50 mins | C20 (Ch 5) |
| 003.02.13 Demonstrate use of beacons. | Demonstrate use of beacons, to include: http; smb; and dns. | Demonstration | 1 x 50 mins | C20 (Chs 4 and 5) |
| 003.02.14 Have candidates practice using beacons. | Candidates will practice using beacons as per LO 003.02.13, based on provided scenarios, to include: http; smb; and dns. | Performance (Lab) | 4 x 50 mins | C20 (Chs 4 and 5) |
| 003.02.15 Explain modifying beacon behaviour. | Explain: Purpose of modifying beacon behaviour (i.e., evading detection and network access controls); and Methods of modifying beacon behaviour, to include: Beaconing time and jitter; and Modulation location (i.e., http header). | Interactive Lecture | 1 x 50 mins | C20 (Chs 5 and 8.3) |
| 003.02.16 Demonstrate modifying beacon behaviour. | Demonstrate modifying beacon behaviour, to include: Beaconing time and jitter; and Beacon protocol use. | Demonstration | 1 x 50 mins | C20 (Chs 5 and 8.3) |
| 003.02.17 Have candidates practice modifying beacon behaviour. | Candidates will practice modifying beacon behaviour as per LO 003.02.16, based on provided scenarios, to include: Beaconing time and jitter; and Beacon protocol use. | Performance (Lab) | 1 x 50 mins | C20 (Chs 5 and 8.3) |

**EO 003.03**

- Performance: Deliver effects by using an attack framework.

- Conditions:

- given:

- analyst workstation;

- analysis software and tools;

- references;

- suitable work area; and

- internet access;

- denied: nil; and

- environment: classroom/computer lab equipped with internet access.

- Standard: IAW refs C1 (Chapters 3, 4, and 8), C3 (Chapter 9), C5, C9 (Chapter 8), C17, C20 (Chapter 8), C21 (Chapters 5–7), C32 and C36, the candidate shall deliver effects by using an attack framework by:

- reading data, to include:

- monitoring network traffic;

- exfiltrating data;

- enumerating local file system; and

- identifying software misconfigurations/vulnerabilities;

- creating or modifying data, to include:

- degrading data integrity;

- injecting network traffic; and

- altering software functionality;

- deleting data, to include:

- destroying information;

- degrading software functionality; and

- destroying software; and

- collecting user generated data, to include:

- keylogging;

- collecting screenshots;

- hijacking devices; and

- conducting browser pivoting.

- Time Required:

- EO – 45 x 50 min periods;

- EC (EC#7) – 8 X 50 min periods (includes time for initial attempt, debriefing and additional practice if required, and retest if required); and

- PC 003 – time allocated at paragraph 4c of EO 003.01.

- Instructional Methods:

- interactive lecture; and

- demonstration – performance.

- Training Aids:

- projector connected to instructor workstation;

- projector screen;

- whiteboard, with markers and eraser; 

- PowerPoint slides containing key information related to lessons (e.g., definitions, diagrams); and

- internet access to the Intel® 64 and IA-32 Architectures Software Developer’s Manual.

- Test Details: See Annex E, Assessment Plan.

- References: C1, C3, C5, C9, C17, C20, C21, C32 and C36.

- Remarks: Nil.

- Teaching Points:

| Lesson Objective (LO) | Teaching Point | Method/ Activities | Time | Refs |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 003.03.01 Explain methods of monitoring network traffic. | Explain methods of monitoring network traffic on a target system, to include: Tcpdump; and Sniffing with a framework. | Interactive Lecture | 1 x 50 mins | C9 (Ch 8) and C17 (Chs 5-7) |
| 003.03.02 Demonstrate monitoring network traffic with an attack framework. | Demonstrate monitoring network traffic on a target system with an attack framework, to include: Tcpdump; and Sniffing with a framework. | Demonstration | 1 x 50 mins | C9 (Ch 8) and C17 (Chs 5-7) |
| 003.03.03 Have candidates practice monitoring network traffic with an attack framework. | Have candidates practice monitoring network traffic with an attack framework as per LO 003.02.02, based on provided scenarios. | Performance (Lab) | 1 x 50 mins | C9 (Ch 8) and C17 (Chs 5-7) |
| 003.03.04 Explain methods of exfiltrating data. | Explain using: Covert channels; and Legitimate protocols (e.g., email, dropbox). | Interactive Lecture | 1 x 50 mins | C3 (Ch 9) |
| 003.03.05 Demonstrate exfiltrating data. | Demonstrate: Overt exfiltration of data; and Use of a covert channel. | Demonstration | 1 x 50 mins | C17 (Ch 6) |
| 003.03.06 Have candidates practice exfiltrating data. | Have candidates practice exfiltrating data with an attack framework as per LO 003.03.05, based on provided scenarios. | Performance (Lab) | 4 x 50 mins | C17 (Ch 6) |
| 003.03.07 Explain enumerating a target file system. | Explain: Choosing what and when to enumerate; and File enumeration techniques, to include: Manual enumeration; and Enumeration with custom scripts. | Interactive Lecture | 1 x 50 mins | C5 and C20 (Ch 8) |
| 003.03.08 Demonstrate enumerating a target file system. | Demonstrate: File enumeration using an attack framework; and Selective file enumeration with a custom tool/script. | Demonstration | 1 x 50 mins | C17 and C20 (Ch 8) |
| 003.03.09 Have candidates practice enumerating target file systems. | Have candidates practice file enumeration using an attack framework as per LO 003.03.08, based on provided scenarios. | Performance (Lab) | 4 x 50 mins | C17 and C20 (Ch 8) |
| 003.03.10 Explain and demonstrate how to identify software misconfigurations. | Explain and demonstrate the methodology used to identify software misconfigurations by examining case studies and real world examples (e.g., misconfigured .ini file, external web proxy misconfiguration). | Interactive Lecture and Demonstration | 2 x 50 mins | C32 (Ch 1) |
| 003.03.11 Have candidates practice identifying software misconfigurations. | Have candidates practice identifying software misconfigurations as per LO 003.03.10, based on provided scenarios. | Performance (Lab) | 4 x 50 mins | C32 (Ch 1) |
| 003.03.12 Explain degrading data integrity. | Explain methods of degrading data integrity, to include: Timestomping; and Modifying file contents. | Interactive Lecture | 1 x 50 mins | C17 (Ch 6) |
| 003.03.13 Demonstrate degrading data integrity. | Demonstrate: Timestomping; and Modifying file contents. | Demonstration | 1 x 50 mins | C17 (Ch 6) |
| 003.03.14 Have candidates practice degrading data integrity. | Have candidates practice degrading data integrity as per LO 003.03.13, based on provided scenarios. | Performance (Lab) | 1 x 50 mins | C17 (Ch 6) |
| 003.03.15 Explain generation of network traffic using a tool. | Explain: Methods of generating network traffic (i.e., using Ettercap, Bettercap); and Attack techniques associated with generating network traffic. | Interactive Lecture | 1 x 50 mins | C1 (Chs 3 and 4) and C5 |
| 003.03.16 Demonstrate network traffic generation using a tool. | Demonstrate: Man-in-the-middle; Denial of Service attack; and Session hijacking. | Demonstration | 3 x 50 mins | C1 (Chs 3 and 4), C9 (Ch 8) and C17 |
| 003.03.17 Have candidates practice generating network traffic using a tool. | Have candidates practice man in the middle attacks using tools (e.g., Ettercap or Bettercap) as per LO 003.03.16, based on provided scenarios. | Performance (Lab) | 2 x 50 mins | C1 (Chs 3 and 4), C9 (Ch 8) and C17 |
| 003.03.18 Explain methods of altering software functionality. | Explain methods of altering existing software functionality, to include: Backdooring a file; and Modifying a binary. | Interactive Lecture | 1 x 50 mins | C36 |
| 003.03.19 Demonstrate altering software functionality. | Demonstrate altering software functionality by backdooring a portable executable (PE) file. | Demonstration | 1 x 50 mins | C36 |
| 003.03.20 Have candidates practice altering software functionality. | Have candidates practice altering software functionality as per LO 003.03.19, based on provided scenarios. | Performance (Lab) | 2 x 50 mins | C36 |
