| 003.03.23 Have candidates practice destroying information and software with an attack framework. | Have candidates practice destroying information/software with an attack framework as per LO 003.03.22, based on provided scenarios. | Performance (Lab) | 1 x 50 mins | C17 (Ch 6) |
| 003.03.24 Explain collecting user generated data. | Explain collecting user generated data, to include: Keylogging; Collecting screenshots; Device hijacking; and Browser pivoting. | Interactive Lecture | 1 x 50 mins | C1 (Ch 8), C5 and C17 (Ch 6) |
| 003.03.25 Demonstrate collecting user generated data with an attack framework. | Demonstrate collecting user generated data (network traffic) using techniques listed in LO 003.03.24. | Demonstration | 3 x 50 mins | C1 (Ch 8), C5 and C17 (Ch 6) |
| 003.03.26 Have candidates practice collecting user generated data with an attack framework. | Have candidates practice collecting user generated data with an attack framework as per LO 003.03.25, based on provided scenarios. | Performance (Lab) | 4 x 50 mins | C1 (Ch 8), C5 and C17 (Ch 6) |

## PO 004 Conduct cyber threat emulation

**EO 004.01**

- Performance: Select offensive cyber effects based upon adversary TTPs.

- Conditions:

- given:

					- analyst workstation;

					- TTPs of an adversary to be emulated;

					- cyber threat brief (e.g., CFNOC Intelligence Product);

					- Red Team SOPs;

					- Red Team Mission Journal (RTMJ);

					- master sequence of events list (MSEL);

					- obfuscation and packer tools;

					- references; and

					- Internet access (Exploit-db.com, CVE Database);

- denied: nil; and

- environment: a closed range environment as part of a simulated exercise to train Blue Team members, or on a simulated production network to practice evaluating Blue Team detection and incident response.

- Standard: IAW refs C16, C17, C20, C28, and C30, the candidate shall select offensive cyber effects based upon adversary TTPs by:

- analysing adversary TTPs in order to ascertain specific effects and artifacts that need to be reproduced, to include identifying:

- the method used by the adversary to gain the initial foothold (e.g., phishing, drive-by download, watering hole, binary exploitation, supply-chain attack, credential abuse, trusted insider, road apples);

- specific Common Vulnerability Exposures (CVEs) exploited in order to gain initial access or move laterally;

- the method used to gain persistence (e.g., scheduled tasks, registry settings, cron job, replacing service executables);

- the location of dropped files, and method of obfuscation (e.g., time stomping, file naming, modifying ownership);

- additional tools (both open source and custom) the adversary may have used;

- the resources used by the adversary to develop custom software (e.g., compiler version, compile time, keyboard mapping, folder structure, programmer’s name);

- the method(s) used for C2 (e.g., http, https, dns, and custom protocols);

- the call-back domains used for C2; and

- the adversary’s objective (e.g., exfiltrate sensitive information, deface web site, degrade capability of Supervisory Control and Data Acquisition [SCADA]);

- selecting appropriate exploits, payloads and tools to achieve similar effects to those observed in the TTPs;

- developing custom C2 profiles to control the network signature of the beacon being delivered to the target;

- developing and/or requesting custom exploits, to emulate the exploitation tactics observed in the TTPs;

- developing and/or requesting custom payloads and tools, to emulate the payloads and tools observed in the TTPs; and

- obfuscating binaries to evade detection, using the appropriate obfuscation methods, as required.

- Time Required:

- EO – 19 x 50 min periods;

- EC (EC#8) – 2 x 50 min periods (includes time for initial attempt, debriefing and additional practice if required, and retest if required); and

- PC 004 – 24 x 50 min periods (includes time for initial attempt, debriefing and additional practice if required, and retest if required).

- Instructional Methods:

- interactive lecture; and

- demonstration – performance.

- Training Aids:

- projector connected to instructor workstation;

- projector screen;

- whiteboard, with markers and eraser; 

- PowerPoint slides containing key information related to lessons (e.g., definitions, diagrams); and

- internet access.

- Test Details: See Annex E, Assessment Plan. 

- Reference: C16, C17, C20, C28 and C30.

- Remarks: 

- the Red Team Analyst will conduct this task in support of an operation or exercise:

- when executing the mission for an exercise, the Red Analyst will be adhering to the MSELs; and

- when executing the mission for an operation, the Red Analyst is constrained by delivering only approved cyber effects;

- the Cyber Threat Brief is produced by the CFNOC Cyber Threat Intelligence Cell (CTIC). The Cyber Threat Brief is a detailed analysis of a particular threat actor, and highlights the TTPs required by the Red Analyst. The Cyber Threat Brief is classified SECRET or TOP SECRET. CTIC will need to be tasked to generate an UNCLASS Cyber Threat Brief to support an UNCLASS training environment;

- under certain circumstances, the candidate will need to select cyber effects based on the requirements identified in an exercise MSEL; and

- internet access is required in order to allow the candidates to conduct open source research for TTPs.

- Teaching Points:

| Lesson Objective (LO) | Teaching Point | Method/ Activities | Time | Refs |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 004.01.01 Provide an introduction to threat emulation. | Explain the purpose of conducting threat emulation, to include: Threat emulation is conducted to validate the Blue Team’s incident response capability, or to demonstrate meaningful business risk due to vulnerabilities or misconfigurations. Threat emulation takes place on production networks, as well as on exercise networks in a training environment. Threat emulation allows the Blue Team to better understand their ability to detect advanced adversaries, and offers insight into incidents that the Blue Team fails to detect. Threat emulation can have adverse effects on the network being attacked, and as such shall only be conducted in a controlled manner by trained Red Analysts. Threat emulation can focus on a named adversary, such as a nation state actor, or be generic in nature and simulate basic attack techniques. | Interactive Lecture | 1 x 50 mins | C20 (Section 1) |
| 004.01.02 Provide a refresher on the main tools used to conduct threat emulation. | Explain and demonstrate the main tools used to conduct threat emulation, to include: Cyber threat emulation is very much focused on the actions taken by the Red Analyst once on the victim machine (e.g., post compromise); Cobalt Strike refresher; Empire refresher; and Meterpreter refresher. Note: Depending upon the candidates’ last exposure to these tools, this lesson may be omitted or expanded. A solid understanding of these tools is required for the practical portion of this EO. | Interactive Lecture and Demonstration | 1 x 50 mins | C17, C20 and C28 |
| 004.01.03 Explain what comprises a TTP. | Explain that: An adversary’s offensive cyber capabilities can be described in terms of the TTPs utilized to achieve desired effects. TTPs can be identified using published reports found on the Internet, referencing intelligence reports such as the Cyber Threat Brief, or developed by the Red Analyst through analysis of adversaries actions. The effects of TTPs (adversary actions) results in indicators of compromise (IOCs) observed on the network and/or host being attacked. It is important for the Red Analyst to also understand the IOCs. | Interactive Lecture | 2 x 50 mins | Nil. |
| 004.01.04 Explain and demonstrate how to analyze TTPs in order to extract the relevant information. | Explain that the Cyber Threat Brief will be used as the primary document from which to draw the TTPs. Demonstrate how to read a Cyber Threat Brief in order to analyze the TTPs and IOCs, to include: the method used by the adversary to gain the initial foothold; CVEs exploited in order to gain initial access; the method used to gain persistence; the location of dropped files, and method of obfuscation; additional tools (both open source and custom) the adversary may have used; the resources used by the adversary to develop custom software; the method(s) used for C2; the call-back domains used for C2; and the adversary’s objective. | Interactive Lecture and Demonstration | 4 x 50 mins | C16 |
| 004.01.05 Explain and demonstrate how to select appropriate exploits, tools and payloads. | Demonstrate how to search for and then select appropriate exploits, tools and payloads to emulate the original TTPs. Explain that sometimes the Red Analyst will need to either develop or request custom exploits, tools and payloads should no appropriate binaries be found. | Interactive Lecture and Demonstration | 2 x 50 mins | C16 |
| 004.01.06 Explain and demonstrate how to develop custom C2 profiles to control the network signature of the beacon being delivered to the target. | C2 profiles are a critical component of threat emulation, in that the network signature can be highly customized to be covert, or mimic already signatured traffic. Demonstrate how to develop a custom C2 profile. | Interactive Lecture and Demonstration | 1 x 50 mins | C30 (Section 12) |
| 004.01.07 Explain the requirement of the Red Analyst to develop and/or request custom exploits, to emulate the exploitation tactics observed in the TTPs. | Explain that: There will be situations where an adversary employed a custom exploit to gain access, and the Red Analyst either does not have access to the original exploit, the original exploit cannot be utilized due to hard-coded dependencies, or a suitable substitution does not exist. When these situations arise, the Red Analyst will need to develop a custom exploit which generates similar IOCs, or identify the requirement to have an exploit developer create the exploit. Note: At this point in their career, the Red Analyst will not likely have the skills or knowledge to develop custom exploits. Exploit development is beyond the scope of this EO, and will be an advanced skill taught to select Red Analysts. | Interactive Lecture | 1 x 50 mins | Nil. |
| 004.01.08 Explain how to develop and/or requesting custom payloads and tools, to emulate the payloads and tools observed in the TTPs. | Explain that: There will be situations where an adversary employed a custom payload or tool to achieve an effect, and the Red Analyst either does not have access to the original binaries, the original payload or tool cannot be utilized due to hard-coded dependencies, or a suitable substitution does not exist. When these situations arise, the Red Analyst will need to develop a custom payload or tool which generates similar IOCs, or identify the requirement to have an exploit developer create the payload or tool. Note: The Red Analyst will have been introduced to payload and tool development in PO 002. | Interactive Lecture | 2 x 50 mins | Nil. |
| 004.01.09 Explain how to obfuscate binaries to evade detection, as required. | Explain: Most adversaries will obfuscate their binaries in an attempt to circumvent Blue Team defences. The specific tools used for obfuscation will depend on the adversary, and the type of binary to be obfuscated. There exists digital forensic tools which can identify the obfuscation tool used to obfuscate a binary, which can be utilized by the Red Analyst should the analyst not know the obfuscation method. Meterpreter has a number of tools which an Analyst can use to obfuscate binaries (e.g., veil-evasion). Note: The Red Analyst will have been introduced to binary obfuscation in PO 003. | Interactive Lecture | 1 x 50 mins | C17 |
| 004.01.10 Have candidates practice analyzing a Cyber Threat Report and then select appropriate exploits, tools and payloads. | Have candidates practice analyzing a provided Cyber Threat Report in order to extract the relevant information and select appropriate exploits, tools and payloads. | Performance (Lab) | 4 x 50 mins | Nil. |

**EO 004.02**

- Performance: Deploy and maintain Red Team infrastructure.

- Conditions:

- given:

					- cyber range;

					- attack framework;

					- secure socket layer (SSL) certificates;

					- analyst workstation;

					- Kali linux ISO for redirector and team server;

					- Red Team SOPs;

					- Red Team Mission Journal (RTMJ);

					- python scripts (setips.py); and

					- references;

- denied: nil; and

- environment: a closed range environment as part of a simulated exercise to train Blue Team members, or on a simulated production network to practice evaluating Blue Team detection and incident response.

- Standard: IAW refs C14 (Chapter 1), C20, C26 and C32, the candidate shall deploy and maintain Red Team infrastructure by:

- selecting and registering call-back domain names, to include:

- selecting appropriate domain names;

- using a script to generate random domain names in bulk;

- testing if the domain name already exists on network;

- creating the appropriate A Records;

- testing access to the new domain using nslookup; and

- recording the actions in the RTMJ;

- deploying and maintaining redirectors, to include:

- deploying the redirector as a Kali linux VM;

- using socat, iptables and the setips.py script to enable forwarding;

- setting up http port redirection using Apache mod_rewrite to obfuscate redirector if using an http beacon; and

- testing access to the redirector to validate connectivity;

- deploying and maintaining team servers, to include:

- deploying the team server as a Kali linux VM;

- uploading the C2 profile to the team server;

- starting the team server; 

- testing access to the team server using Cobalt Strike; and

- documenting the team servers ip address, hash and password in the RTMJ;

- generating and deploying SSL certificates for https beacons; and

- deploying and maintaining defensive tools, services, decoys and malware on Red Team Infrastructure to deny Blue Team’s ability to gain intelligence or access during their incident response, to include:

- hardening Red Team infrastructure servers by closing unused ports and disabling unnecessary services;

- deploying web-hooks (BeEF Hook) on attacker controlled web servers; and

- deploying Apache mod_rewrite to forward web traffic to an intermediary web server.

- Time Required:

- EO – 11 x 50 min periods;

- EC – nil; and

- PC 004 – time allocated at para 4c of EO 004.01.

- Instructional Methods:

- interactive lecture; and

- demonstration – performance.

- Training Aids:

- projector connected to instructor workstation;

- projector screen;

- whiteboard, with markers and eraser; and

- PowerPoint slides containing key information related to lessons (e.g., definitions, diagrams).

- Test Details: See Annex E, Assessment Plan. 

- References: C14, C20, C26 and C32.

- Remarks: Nil.

- Teaching Points:

| Lesson Objective (LO) | Teaching Point | Method/ Activities | Time | Refs |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 004.02.01 Explain Red Team infrastructure. | Explain: The significance of maintaining Red Team Infrastructure; The standard naming convention used for each server (i.e., staging, long haul, active); and The importance of documenting all configurations and changes, and having these documents on a shared drive accessible by all team members. | Interactive Lecture | 1 x 50 mins | C14, C20, C26 and C32 |
| 004.02.02 Explain and demonstrate how to select and register call-back domain names. | Explain and demonstrate: The Top Level Domain (TLDs) to be included in gray space (e.g., .com, .net, .org, .ca); Selecting appropriate domain names; Using a script to randomly generate domain names; Registering the A Record for each new domain name; Testing access to each domain name using nslookup; and Recording the changes in the Red Team Mission Journal (RTMJ). | Interactive Lecture and Demonstration | 1 x 50 mins | Nil. |
| 004.02.03 Explain and demonstrate how to set up redirectors. | Explain and demonstrate: Installing the redirector VM as a Kali linux VM; Using socat, iptables and setips.py script to enable forwarding; Setting up http port redirection using Apache mod_rewrite to obfuscate redirector if using an http beacon; and Testing access to the redirector to validate connectivity. | Interactive Lecture and Demonstration | 1 x 50 mins | C14, C20 and C26 |
| 004.02.04 Explain and demonstrate how to set up team servers. | Explain and demonstrate: Deploying the team server as a Kali linux VM; Uploading the C2 profile to the team server; Starting the team server; Testing access to the team server using Cobalt Strike; and Documenting the team server’s IP address, hash, and password in the RTMJ. | Interactive Lecture and Demonstration | 1 x 50 mins | C14, C20 and C26 |
| 004.02.05 Explain and demonstrate how to generate an SSL certificate for https beacons. | Explain and demonstrate: Generating an SSL certificate; and Installing SSL certificate into Cobalt Strike’s Team Server. | Interactive Lecture and Demonstration | 1 x 50 mins | C14, C20 and C26 |
| 004.02.06 Explain and demonstrate how to deploy and maintain defensive tools on the Red Team infrastructure. | Explain and demonstrate: During the Blue Team’s incident response process, they will likely visit domains controlled by the Red Team, which are hosting payloads and exploits; The Red Analyst is highly motivated to both obfuscate the true purpose of the web site, as well as deny the Blue Team access to Red Team tools; Hardening the Red Team infrastructure servers by closing unused ports and disabling unnecessary services; Deploying web-hooks (BeEF Hook) on attacker controlled web servers; and Deploying Apache mod_rewrite to forward web traffic to an intermediary web server. | Interactive Lecture and Demonstration | 2 x 50 mins | C32 |
| 004.02.07 Have candidates practice deploying and hardening Red Team infrastructure. | Candidates will practice deploying and hardening Red Team infrastructure as per LO 004.02.06, based on provided scenarios. | Performance (Lab) | 4 x 50 mins | C26 and C32 |

**EO 004.03**

- Performance: Deliver offensive cyber effects.

- Conditions:

- given:

					- cyber range;

					- attack framework;

					- SSL certificates;

					- analyst workstation;

					- TTPs of adversary to be emulated;

					- cyber threat brief (CFNOC Intelligence Product);

					- Red Team SOPs;

					- Red Team Mission Journal (RTMJ);

					- references; and

					- MSEL;

- denied: nil; and

- environment: a closed range environment as part of a simulated exercise to train Blue Team members, or on a simulated production network to practice evaluating Blue Team detection and incident response.

- Standard: IAW ref B2, the candidate shall deliver offensive cyber effects by:

- coordinating with the exercise Team Controller (TC) to synchronize the delivery of offensive cyber effects with the MSEL timeline and Blue Team preparedness (only when delivering threat emulation in support of an exercise);

- emulating the threat on an exercise or on operation as part of a Red Team, to include:

- analyzing adversary TTPs and developing a plan;

- deploying Red Team infrastructure;

- gaining initial access to the network;

- moving laterally within the network;

- passing access to team members;

- spawning new access on different Team Servers; and

- achieving mission objectives;

- recording all pertinent operational information in the RTMJ, to include command, IP address, mac address, hostname, user name, analyst name, date time group, and comments;

- analyzing metadata, network traffic and logs, which is required to assist the Red Analyst assess the effectiveness of their attack, as well as provide supporting data during the Blue Team debrief; and

- maintaining an inventory of all delivered binaries, documents, and crafted packets.

- Time Required:

- EO – 10 x 50 min periods;

- EC – nil; and

- PC 004 – time allocated at para 4c of EO 004.01.

- Instructional Methods:

- interactive lecture; and

- performance.

- Training Aids:

- projector connected to instructor workstation;

- projector screen;

- whiteboard, with markers and eraser;

- PowerPoint slides containing key information related to lessons (e.g., definitions, diagrams); and

- internet access.

- Test Details: See Annex E, Assessment Plan.

- Reference: B2.

- Remarks:

- effective threat emulation is a function of realistically emulating an adversary’s actions on the network. In many situations, the assumption of “assumed compromise” is adopted, and therefore the first step of establishing the initial foothold is provided as the start state. It is accepted that given enough time (and money), an adversary will find a way to breach a network. During an ‘assumed compromise’ mission, the Red Analyst achieves the training objective through moving laterally within the network and employing tools in the same manner that the adversary would;

- a Red Team mission is in support of a training objective. It is crucial that the Red Analyst understands that the delivery of cyber effects must be controlled via MSELs (on exercise) or pre-approved and only released under authority (on a production network); and

- a Red Team mission is conducted by a team. Consequently, an appropriate tool will be used (e.g., Cobalt Strike) allowing the Red Analyst to participate in a cyber engagement as a team member. Team members will be required to pass access (beacons) back and forth amongst team members.

- Teaching Points:

| Lesson Objective (LO) | Teaching Point | Method/ Activities | Time | Refs |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 004.03.01 Introduce delivering offensive cyber effects. | Explain that: Threat emulation is the culmination of Red Team training, and positions the analyst to deliver cyber effects under constraining conditions. Up to this point, candidates have been taught how to select and deliver a cyber effect. Now, they will deliver their selected cyber effects using an appropriate attack framework in a team environment. Threat emulation takes place on production networks and on exercise. | Interactive Lecture | 1 x 50 mins | B2 |
| 004.03.02 Explain the purpose of the MSEL. | Explain: The MSEL is a tool used during exercises which consists of an approved schedule of cyber effects to be delivered. The effects are selected to achieve the desired level of validation for the Blue Team. The exercise TC is overall responsible for the exercise conduct, and may choose to alter the MSELs to ensure maximum training value is delivered to the Primary Training Audience (PTA), often the Blue Team defenders. The exercise TC may choose to postpone, advance, remove or amend any serial of the MSEL. The Red Analyst is responsible to ensure best effort is made to ensure delivery of an effect in accordance with the MSEL, which may require a significant amount of preparatory work (e.g., preparing the content of a phishing email, as well as the exploit and payload prior to launching a phishing campaign). | Interactive Lecture | 3 x 50 mins | Nil. |
| 004.03.03 Explain recording operational information in the Red Team Mission Journal (RTMJ). | Explain that: The Red Analyst shall record all information from both the attacker and the victim for each delivered attack (i.e., IP address, mac address, hostname, exploit, payload, port, etc.), within the RTMJ. An inventory of all delivered binaries, documents and crafted packets must be maintained within the RTMJ. | Interactive Lecture | 1 x 50 mins | Red Team SOPs |
| 004.03.04 Explain analyzing metadata, network traffic and logs. | Explain that analyzing metadata, network traffic and logs is required to assist the Red Analyst assess the effectiveness of their attack, as well as provide supporting data during the Blue Team debrief. The candidate would have already been taught these skills, the difference now is that they will monitor the tools while delivering a cyber effect. | Interactive Lecture | 1 x 50 mins | Nil. |
| 004.03.05 Have candidates practice delivering offensive cyber effects. | Have candidates practice using attack frameworks to deliver effects in the context of a Red Team, based on provided scenarios/MSELs. Note: For the lab, there must be more than two Team Servers running (with different roles, such as staging, long haul) because candidates will practice spawning access from one Team Server to another. | Performance (Lab) | 4 x 50 mins | As per EO 004.03 |

**EO 004.04**

- Performance: Conduct post-exercise drills.

- Conditions:

- given:

					- cyber range;

					- attack framework;

					- SSL certificates;

					- analyst workstation;

					- TTPs of adversary to be emulated;

					- cyber threat brief (CFNOC Intelligence Product);

					- Red Team SOPs;

					- Red Team Mission Journal (RTMJ);

					- references; and

					- MSEL;

- denied: nil; and

- environment: a closed range environment as part of a simulated exercise to train Blue Team members, or on a simulated production network to practice evaluating Blue Team detection and incident response.

- Standard: IAW ref B2 and C13, the candidate shall conduct post-exercise drills by:

- debriefing the Blue Team on the serialized list of offensive cyber effects delivered, the success or failure of each attack, as well as detection methods the Blue Team can employ to observe the Indicators of Compromise (IOC) created by each attack;

- recommending cyber incident recovery measures required to restore the network under attack to pre-attack condition; and

- safeguarding or destroying all exfiltrated client data obtained during the execution of the mission.

- Time Required:

- EO – 6 x 50 min periods;

- EC (EC#9) – 16 x 50 min periods (includes time for initial attempt, debriefing and additional practice if required, and retest if required); and

- PC 004 – time allocated at para 4c of EO 004.01.

- Instructional Methods:

- interactive lecture; and

- demonstration.

- Training Aids:

- projector connected to instructor workstation;

- projector screen;

- whiteboard, with markers and eraser; and

- PowerPoint slides containing key information related to lessons (e.g., definitions, diagrams).

- Test Details: See Annex E, Assessment Plan.

- References: B2 and C13.

- Remarks:

- where feasible, have the candidates deliver attacks against an exercise network being monitored by an actual Blue Team, and then use the Blue Team’s defensive tools during the post-exercise debrief. Although the resources required for this level of confirmation would be significant, the results would justify the additional effort; and

- the EC for EO 004.04 (EC#9) immediately precedes PC 004, which encompasses all four EOs and spans three days. The lab should be designed to include identifying an adversary to emulate, selecting exploits, payloads and tools, following a pre-designed MSEL, and requiring the candidates to conduct their mission as a team.

- Teaching Points:

| Lesson Objective (LO) | Teaching Point | Method/ Activities | Time | Refs |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 004.04.01 Explain what is involved in the post-exercise de-brief. | Explain: The value derived from conducting threat emulation is the feedback provided by the Red Analyst to the Blue Team in the form of a post exercise de-brief. For operations that last longer than a single day, a shorter post-exercise debrief is often conducted at the end of each day. Emphasize that the mentality should NOT be seen as Red against Blue; rather, the Red team is a friendly asset used to assist in training the Blue Team to improve their defensive tradecraft. An adversarial stance between the two teams will erode trust and respect. During an exercise, the Blue Team will be recording their observations in a log so that the Red and Blue events can be cross-referenced as a means to assess Blue’s detection effectiveness. During a Penetration Test, the Blue Team will likely not be aware of the Red Team actions, and thus their observations will be recorded in their normal Incident Response report. It is critical that the Red Analyst understands the effects they generate on the target network, as well as how the Blue Team can most effectively detect the attack. | Interactive Lecture | 1 x 50 mins | Ref C13 is an example of a document which can be leveraged to understand how the Blue Team can detect lateral movement. |
| 004.04.02 Explain and demonstrate how to brief the Blue Team. | Explain and demonstrate how to: Describe the overall mission of the Red Team, to include a high level overview of the adversary, the mission, the adversary’s motivation and TTPs utilized; Chronologically iterate through the delivered attacks, and describe the attack vector used, challenges, successes and failures; When practical, demonstrate the attack using the same attack framework used for the initial attack, and have a Blue Team Analyst actually look for the attack using their own defensive tools. This is proven to be one of the most effective methods the Red Analyst can use to ensure Blue Team understanding, and offers the best training value; Be sure to avoid harsh criticism, and instead identify areas for improvement; and Provide an opportunity for the Blue Team to ask questions, and ensure to provide accurate answers. | Interactive Lecture and Demonstration | 2 x 50 mins | C13 |
| 004.04.03 Explain and demonstrate how to recommend appropriate cyber incident recovery measures. | Explain and demonstrate: The actions necessary to return the network to its pre-attack state: When Threat Emulation has been authorized on a production network, it is imperative to return the network to its pre-attack state upon completion of the Red Team mission. However, in most circumstances, exercise networks will not have this requirement; The Red Team Mission Journal will be used to identify all actions taken against the victim network, which is provided to the Blue Team; Advise the Blue Team on the most effective means of returning the network to pre-attack condition, which is highly dependent upon the type of attack and the effect achieved; and Advise the Blue Team on how to verify that their efforts to return the network to pre-attack condition were successful. | Interactive Lecture and Demonstration | 2 x 50 mins | Nil. |
| 004.04.04 Explain safe handling of client data. | Explain: During the conduct of a Red Team mission on a production network, there exists a high level of risk that the Red Analyst will acquire sensitive data, either through the process of collecting traffic or exfiltrating data. It is imperative that in order to maintain a high level of trust and respect, that the Red Analyst safeguard all collected data according to Red Team SOPs. Failure to safeguard client data will result in a breach of trust between the client and the Red Team, and may include disciplinary actions. | Interactive Lecture | 1 x 50 mins | B2 |

