# ANNEX E – ASSESSMENT PLAN

| **PC/****EC** | **PO/EO Description** | **Test Type** | **Test Purpose** | **Time** **Allotted** | **When** | **Test Description** | **Resources** | **Impact** |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **EC #1** 001.01 | Conduct passive cyber reconnaissance. | Practical | Formative: To assess the candidate’s ability to conduct passive reconnaissance. | 4 x 50 mins | On completion of EO 001.01. | The candidates will be given an organization or entity (e.g., 4 Division) to profile. They will be assessed on their ability to gather information about the target organization that would be useful in developing an active reconnaissance plan or enabling other actions (e.g., generating password lists). Properly identifying IP ranges, DNS servers, mail servers and key positions (e.g., technical contacts, domain admins) will be worth a predetermine amount of points. Targets will be real organisations and must have profiles that lend themselves to the assigned tasks for the candidates. | Open source attack framework, internet access, and access to appropriate printed resources desired by the candidate. | Candidates who are unsuccessful will be debriefed and given additional training and mentoring as required. |
| **EC #2** 001.02 | Conduct active cyber reconnaissance. | Practical | Formative: To assess the candidate’s ability to conduct active reconnaissance. | 4 x 50 mins | On completion of EO 001.02. | The candidates will be given an attack framework and a cyber range. The range will consist of five target machines with different attack vectors. Candidates must be able to scan the target machines and gather information about the services offered. They will be assessed on their ability to properly identify services. Identified services on each target will be worth a predetermined point value. A predetermined number of additional points will be given if a candidate is able to provide detailed information about a given service (e.g., version of software running). Targets will be a mix of Windows and Linux workstations and servers. Services will either be default installations, have misconfiguration errors that lead to information gathering, have poorly configured access controls, or have poor practices employed (e.g., SQL port open to connections from external IP addresses). | Open source attack framework, applicable targets and network infrastructure, internet access, and access to appropriate printed resources desired by the candidate. | Candidates who are unsuccessful will be debriefed and given additional training and mentoring as required. |
| **EC #3** 001.03 | Assess extent of identified vulnerabilities. | Practical | Formative: To assess the candidate’s ability to assess the extent of identified vulnerabilities. | 2 x 50 mins | On completion of EO 001.03. | The candidates will be given an attack framework and a cyber range. The range will consist of five target machines with different attack vectors. Candidates will be given detailed information about the services offered (could be taken from results of EC #2). Candidates will be assessed on their ability to properly identify possible attack surfaces based on described services. Correctly identified attack surfaces on each target will be worth a predetermined point value. A predetermined number of additional points will be given if a candidate is able to identify attack surfaces that will lead to remote privileged access. Targets will be a mix of Windows and Linux workstations and servers. Services will include well-known vulnerabilities with vary degrees of severity. | Open source attack framework, applicable targets and network infrastructure, internet access, and access to appropriate printed resources desired by the candidate. | Candidates who are unsuccessful will be debriefed and given additional training and mentoring as required. |
| **PC 001** | Conduct cyber reconnaissance. | Practical | Summative: To assess the candidate’s ability to conduct passive and active reconnaissance and assess the extent of identified vulnerabilities. | 4 x 50 mins | On completion of PO 001. | The candidates will be given an attack framework and a cyber range. The range will consist of five target machines with different attack vectors. Candidates will be able to scan the target machines and gather information about the services offered. They will be assessed on their ability to properly identify services offered and identify possible attack surfaces based on found services. Services identified as possible attack surfaces on each target will be worth a predetermined point value. A predetermined number of additional points will be given if a candidate is able to provide detailed information about a given service and identify attack surfaces that will lead to remote privileged access. Targets will be mix of Windows and Linux workstations and servers. Services will either be default installations, have misconfiguration errors that lead to information gathering, have poorly configured access controls, or have poor practices employed (e.g., SQL port open to connections from external IP addresses). Services will include well-known vulnerabilities with varying degrees of severity. | Open source attack framework, applicable targets and network infrastructure, internet access, and access to appropriate printed resources desired by the candidate. | Unsuccessful candidates will be debriefed on their performance and what they missed and provided feedback on how they can improve. They will be given additional mentoring and instruction before their next retest. The retest will use the same format, with a different scenario. Failure to pass the retest will result in cease training. |
| **Critical ** **EC #4 ** 002.01 and 002.02 | Gain access to a target device or network environment. | Practical | Summative: To assess the candidate’s ability to gain initial access and expand access with an attack framework and other integrated tools. | 8 x 50 mins (test will run from morning into the evening) | On completion of EOs 002.01, 002.02, and 003.01. | The candidates will be given an attack framework and a cyber range. The range will consist of five target machines with different attack vectors. Candidates will be able to gain initial access to each target and escalate privileges. They will be assessed on their ability to gain access, expand access, and escalate privileges. User access on each target will be worth a predetermined point value. A predetermined number of additional points will be given if a candidate is able to escalate privileges to administrator. Targets will be a mix of Windows and Linux workstations and servers. Services will either be vulnerable to public exploits, have misconfiguration errors that lead to remote code execution, have poorly configured authentication mechanisms, or have poor password implementation. Privilege escalation techniques should be limited to those taught in EO 002.02. | Open source attack framework, applicable targets and network infrastructure, internet access, and access to appropriate printed resources desired by the candidate. | Candidates who are unsuccessful on this critical EC will be debriefed and given additional practice and mentoring. They will then be retested. Failure to pass the retest will result in cease training. |
| **EC #5 ** 002.04 | Exploit web applications. | Practical | Formative: To assess the candidate’s ability to perform basic web exploitation. | 8 x 50 mins | On completion of EO 002.04. | The candidates will be given an attack framework and a cyber range. They will be given a series of web applications with intentional security vulnerabilities that can be discovered in a simple and straightforward manner (i.e., SQL injection is not blind, an apostrophe will display verbose errors). Candidates will need to successfully leverage these web exploits to achieve the objectives specified in the test. | Open source attack framework, applicable targets and network infrastructure, internet access, and access to appropriate printed resources desired by the candidate. | Candidates who are unsuccessful will be debriefed and given additional training and mentoring as required. |
| **PC 002** | Gain access to a target device or network environment. | Practical | Summative: To assess candidate’s ability to gain initial access and expand access with an attack framework and other integrated tools. | 8 x 50 mins (test will run from morning into the evening) | On completion of PO 002. | The candidates will be given an attack framework and a cyber range. The range will emulate an enterprise network environment with different attack vectors. They will be assigned specific objectives which require them to gain access and escalate privileges on specified hosts within the domain. Candidates will be assessed on their ability to gain access, expand access, and escalate privileges. Completion of specified objectives will be worth a predetermined point value. Targets will be a mix of Windows and Linux workstations and servers. Services will either be vulnerable to public exploits, have misconfiguration errors that lead to remote code execution, have poorly configured authentication mechanisms, be intentionally vulnerable web apps, or have poor password implementation. Privilege escalation techniques should be limited to those taught in EO 002.02. Vulnerable web applications should have vulnerabilities that can easily be found. One of the objective exploitation paths will require implementation of a social engineering activity (i.e., phishing email). This network should attempt to accurately reflect a DND enterprise domain. This includes the use of automated defences and network access controls (i.e., deep packet inspection, Anti-virus, IPS, etc.). | Open source attack framework, applicable targets and network infrastructure, internet access, and access to appropriate printed resources desired by the candidate. | Unsuccessful candidates will be debriefed on their performance and what they missed and provided feedback on how they can improve. They will be given additional mentoring and instruction before their next retest. The retest will use the same format, with a different scenario. Failure to pass the retest will result in cease training. |
| **EC #6** 003.01 | Establish a foothold on a target network through use of an attack framework. | Theory | Formative: To assess the candidate’s comprehension of the knowledge elements covered in EO 003.01. | 2 x 50 mins | On completion of EO 003.01. | Candidates will be given a series of short answer and multiple choice questions that assess the technical knowledge associated with this EO, to include: Operating system (OS) boot mechanisms; OS persistence mechanisms; OS security controls; Memory injection techniques; and Counter DFIR techniques and procedures. | Test, answer key, and PP&S. | Candidates who are unsuccessful will be debriefed and given additional training and mentoring. Candidates will be retested until successful. |
| **EC #7** 003.01 003.03 | Establish a foothold on a target network through use of an attack framework. | Practical | Formative: To assess the candidate’s ability to conduct actions on the objective in compliance with OPSEC principles. | 8 x 50 mins | On completion of PO 003 EOs. | The candidates will be given an attack framework and a cyber range. The range will emulate an enterprise network environment with different attack vectors. Candidates will be assigned specific objectives which require them to gain access and may require escalation of privileges on specified hosts within the domain. Completion of specified objectives will be worth a predetermined point value. They will be penalized for OPSEC violations and detection under specific circumstances. Targets will be mix of Windows and Linux workstations and servers. | Open source attack framework, applicable targets and network infrastructure, internet access, and access to appropriate printed resources desired by the candidate. | Unsuccessful candidates will be debriefed on their performance and what they missed and provided feedback on how they can improve. They will be given additional mentoring and training as required. |
| **PC** **003** | Establish a foothold on a target network through use of an attack framework. | Practical | Summative: To assess the candidate’s ability to conduct actions on the objective in compliance with OPSEC principles. | 8 x 50 mins (test will run from morning into the evening) | On completion of PO 003. | The candidates will be given an attack framework and a cyber range. The range will emulate an enterprise network environment with different attack vectors. Candidates will be assigned specific objectives which require them to gain access and may require escalation of privileges on specified hosts within the domain. Completion of specified objectives will be worth a predetermined point value. Candidates will be penalized for OPSEC violations and detection under specific circumstances. Targets will be mix of Windows and Linux workstations and servers. | Open source attack framework, applicable targets and network infrastructure, internet access, and access to appropriate printed resources desired by the candidate. | Unsuccessful candidates will be debriefed on their performance and what they missed and provided feedback on how they can improve. They will be given additional mentoring and instruction before their next retest. The retest will use the same format, with a different scenario. Failure to pass the retest will result in cease training. |
| **EC#8** 004.01 | Analyze TTPs and select cyber effects. | Practical | Formative: To assess the candidate’s ability to analyze a cyber threat brief to determine TTPs and select appropriate payloads and tools. | 2 x 50 mins | On completion of EO 004.01. | The candidates will be given a Cyber Threat Brief for a fictitious or real named adversary. They are expected to analyze the brief in order to identify the TTPs used by the adversary, and then select appropriate exploits, payloads and tools. They must also identify what actions the adversary would likely take on the network. | Cyber Threat Brief, Internet access (exploit-db), and attack framework. | Unsuccessful candidates will be debriefed on their performance and what they missed and provided feedback on how they can improve. They will be given additional mentoring and training as required. |
| **EC#9** 004.04 | Conduct post-exercise drills. | Practical | Formative: To assess the candidate’s ability to conduct Red Team operations. | 16 x 50 mins (test will run from the first morning into the second evening) | On completion of EO 004. | EC#9 and the PC 004 shall be executed over five continuous days. The first two days represent EC#9, and the final three days represent PC 004. Common to both EC#9 and PC 004: The candidates will be given an attack framework and a cyber range. The range will emulate an enterprise network environment with different attack vectors. During the exercise candidates will be rotated through and assessed on every position in a Red Team. EC#9 - Day One: Candidates will be assigned a specific named actor with TTPs which they must emulate. Candidates will select appropriate exploits, payloads and tools, as well as apply obfuscation and persistence techniques based on the TTPs. They will prepare their Red Infrastructure (i.e., redirectors, team servers, C2 profiles). EC#9 - Day Two: Candidates will be provided objectives which require them to gain access and may require escalation of privileges on specified hosts within the domain. Completion of specified objectives will be worth a predetermined point value. During day two, the network under attack will be actively monitored by a Blue Team. | Cyber Threat Brief, open source attack framework, applicable targets and network infrastructure, internet access, access to appropriate printed resources desired by the candidate, and Blue Team defenders defending the network under attack. | Unsuccessful candidates will be debriefed on their performance and what they missed and provided feedback on how they can improve. They will be given additional mentoring and training as required. |
| **PC** **004** | Conduct threat emulation. | Practical | Summative: To assess the candidate’s ability to conduct threat emulation. | 24 x 50 mins (test will run from the first morning into the third evening) | On completion of PO 004 EOs. | Common to both EC#9 and PC 004: The candidates will be given an attack framework and a cyber range. The range will emulate an enterprise network environment with different attack vectors. During the exercise candidates will be rotated through and assessed on every position in a Red Team. PC 004 - Days One and Two: Candidates will be given a new Cyber Threat Brief, and will start the process of selecting TTPs over again. The Red Team Infrastructure will need to be updated with the new domain names, C2 profiles, etc. Days One and Two will simulate a typical cyber exercise with the candidates attacking a defended network, utilizing the skills and knowledge they acquired throughout this course. PC 004 - Day Three: Candidates will take turns debriefing the Blue Team on their actions, as well as how the Blue Team could have more effectively identified them in the network. | Cyber Threat Brief, open source attack framework, applicable targets and network infrastructure, internet access, access to appropriate printed resources desired by the candidate, and Blue Team defenders defending the network under attack. | Unsuccessful candidates will be debriefed on their performance and what they missed and provided feedback on how they can improve. They will be given additional mentoring and instruction before their next retest. The retest will use the same format, with a different scenario. Failure to pass the retest will result in cease training. |

Legend:

Test Types – Theory or Practical (IAW CFITES Vol 7)

Assessment Purpose – **F**ormative or **S**ummative (IAW CFITES Vol 7); what is the candidate being assessed on (ie, assess their ability to…)?

Test Description – May include details about the testing event’s intent, minimum standard for pass i.e. 70%, or P/F checklist, individual or group testing, etc.  It should contain enough details to ensure that the intent of the test is not missed during the development phase.

