# ANNEX G- RECORD OF DECISIONS

| **Title** | **Red Analyst QSP Writing Board** |
| --- | --- |
| **Location of Board** | **JIIFC, CFS Leitrim, Ottawa ON** |
| **Dates** | **16 Jul – 3 Aug 18** |
| **Board Composition** |
|  | Surname | Initials | Rank | Unit |
| Co-Chair (process) | Gillan | DL | Maj | DG Cyber/D Cyber Ops FD |
| Co-Chair (process) | Forget | JDY | Capt | MPGTG HQ |
| Co-Chair (content) | Tobias | K | Civ | CFNOC |
| SME | Machnee | J | Capt | CFSCE |
| SME | Arndt | WA | MWO | CFNOC |
| SME | Rimpilainen | A | MCpl | CFSCE |
| SME | Charbonneau | J | MCpl | CFSCE |
| SME | Wight | K | Cpl | CFNOC |
| Advisor | Smith | R | Dr | RMC Computer Security Lab |

| **SER #** | **DETAILS** | **ACTION** |
| --- | --- | --- |
|  | The QSPWB was convened at 0800 hours, 16 Jul 18. The Co-Chair (Process) welcomed the board members and provided some background information on the way ahead for the CYBER OP occupation and associated training. They also explained the board members’ responsibilities and the expected deliverables for the week (i.e., revised tasks, completed QSP and detailed RoDs), explained the format of the JBS and associated master task list (MTL), and provided an overview of the Analysis process the board would be following. The importance of including a high level of detail and identifying pertinent references was stressed. | Nil. |
|  | The QSPWB reviewed the job description and associated tasks for the Red Analyst. Serials 3 to 8 outline discussions that arose around the job description and tasks and recommended changes.  The following criteria were applied when revising existing tasks or developing new tasks to confirm their validity: The task is a logical, discrete, and necessary unit of work within a job. The task is performed by an individual (you can “task” someone to do it). The task is observable and measurable. The task has one action verb, one object, and sometimes a qualifier. The task is a specific act done for its own sake, leading to a key accomplishment on the job. The task has an identifiable beginning and end. The tasks occurs over a quantifiable amount of time. Completion of the task is noteworthy as an accomplishment on the job, in terms of a service completed and/or product produced by the job incumbent. | D Cyber Ops FD Trg Dev Advisor to staff required amendments to the Occ Authority (DMPPPC 4, for A/CMP) for onward submission to DPGR Spec Maintenance. |
|  | The board recommended the following changes to the job description for Red Analyst:  “The Red Analyst is an intermediate level LS/Cpl - OS/Pte/Avr job. The job is performed in an operations centre in support of the Sea, Land and Air elements. The primary responsibility is to conduct network vulnerability assessments (Note 1) **adversary-specific cyber threat emulation**. The main tasks include: identify (Note 2) **analyze** client **target** environment; research and map authorized (Note 3) **target **network; identify network**/host** vulnerabilities; penetrate and gain access to network/host **environment** (Note 4); modify and exfiltrate host data **deliver post-exploitation effects** (Note 5); provide recommendations to return target hosts and network to pre-analysis conditions; and author technical reports to include any mitigation recommendations. Note 1: Conducting vulnerability assessments is only one aspect of the job, not the primary one (which is adversary emulation). Note 2: Identifying the target environment is not done by the Red Analyst – they are advised which environment they are to target. Note 3: Authorization is implied when they are provided with the network they are to research and map. Note 4: They will be gaining access beyond the network, to include the broader environment in which it is situated. Note 5: Modifying and exfiltrating data is too limiting as it is only one of several post-exploitation effects. | As per Serial #2 |
|  | The board recommended that the following task be removed from the CYBER OP MTL: BT0320 - Conduct cyber defence audits: Task is redundant, as the role CYBER OPs will play when it comes to audits is covered in more specific tasks). Other audit-related functions (e.g., policy aspects) are outside of the scope of the CYBER OP. | As per Serial #2 |
|  | The board recommended that the following six tasks related to exploit development be removed from the Red Analyst task list. The level of complexity associated with these tasks is outside scope of Red Analyst, and there is a high risk of skill fade due to there being no regular opportunities to practice/employ the associated skillsets. These tasks will remain associated with the Snr Red Analyst, however: FT0080, Troubleshoot toolset software deficiencies FT0090, Determine software deficiencies FT0280, Develop custom network testing toolsets FT0290, Test custom network assessment tool functionality FT0310, Develop custom network exploitation tools FT0320, Test custom exploitation tool functionality The board recommended that the following additional tasks be removed from the task list for Red Analyst: GT0100, Assess technical risk and vulnerability of cyber systems: Assessing technical risk is a senior level function - the Red Analyst will contribute to the process, but will not carry out the assessment. GT0120, Assess non-technical risk and vulnerability of cyber systems: Assessing non-technical risk is a senior level function - the Red Analyst will contribute to the process, but will not carry out the assessment. GT0150, Determine adversary tactics, techniques and procedures (TTPs): This is not a Red Team function (function of the Blue Team, incident handling, forensics, etc.). GT0160, Evaluate adversary TTPs: This task is outside the scope of a junior Red Analyst. | As per Serial #2 |
|  | The board recommended that the following tasks be added to the task list for Red Analyst: ET0050, Collect intrusion artifacts (e.g., source code, malware, and trojans): Red Analysts will be building, developing, modifying, etc., different artifacts that need to be catalogued. FT0380, Maintain deployable cyber defense audit toolkit: This is an essential task for any operator who is using a toolset. | As per Serial #2 |
|  | The board recommended the following new tasks: Support social engineering operations: This is a method of penetrating a network using the human element or aspect as a conduit or vector. Validate the effectiveness of adversary cyber capabilities: Assessment (see BT0310) and validation are two separate tasks performed by the Red Team. Control tool indicators: This is a key Red Team function not reflected in any other tasks. Determine tool indicators: This is a function of senior Red Team members not reflected in any other task. Safeguard client data: Sensitive information may be removed from client systems during operations and must be protected IAW directives, etc. This does not require chain of custody as forensic evidence does (ref task ET0020). Modify existing tool and exploit source code: Modification is not reflected in any existing tasks. | As per Serial #2 |
|  | The board recommended the following modifications to tasks:  BT0260, Attempt to access a target device/network: The desired end state is to gain access, not only to attempt to do so; "Environment" is a more all-encompassing term for the targets they will analyze (e.g., personnel). REVISE to: **Gain** access to a target device/network **environment**”.  BT0310, Assess the effectiveness of adversary cyber capabilities on CAF operational capability: Assessing effects on CAF capabilities is an Intelligence function; the Red Team's assessment will inform that overall assessment. REVISE to: “Assess the effectiveness of adversary cyber capabilities”.  FT0290, Test custom network assessment functionality:  They are not assessing the functionality of the network assessment, but the functionality of the tools employed during the assessment. REVISE task statement to: “Test custom network assessment **tool **functionality”. | As per Serial #2 |
| **QUALIFICATION STANDARD DEVELOPMENT** |
|  | The board conducted an initial train/no-train analysis of the revised task list on Day 2 (17 Jul 18). Training priorities were assigned to the “train” tasks (see Annex B, Task Disposition). The following training priority (TP) level descriptions were applied:  High TP: Over-train to ensure retention and to minimize the likelihood of deterioration of performance or skill fade over time.  Moderate TP: Instruction up to the level required to do the task competently, assuming that task performance follows soon after completion of the instructional programme. Low TP: Learner needs a general familiarity with the task and will become proficient on the job. | Nil. |
|  | The board continued their analysis by identifying the broader functional “work areas” and associated tasks, skills and knowledge for this job. To inform this process, the course outlines and learning outcomes for RMC’s Exploitation Techniques Course were reviewed. Scalar development began on Day 3 (18 Jul 18) and continued into Day 4 (19 Jul 18). Once tasks were organized and grouped into POs, each task was further analyzed to determine any sub-tasks, steps in processes, etc. The rest of Day 4 and Day 5 (20 Jul 18) were dedicated to writing POs, identifying relevant reference material, cross-referencing the POs with the task list, documenting required amendments to the MTL, and finalizing the QS portion of the QSP. | Nil. |
|  | BT0250, Conduct penetration testing: This task not reflected specifically in one area or PO as it applies in many (i.e., 001, 002 and 003). This task encompasses many analysis activities and it is difficult to discretely position it in one aspect of the PO standards. The Red Analyst will focus on enterprise networks only. The Snr Red Analyst will also focus on enterprise networks, but may also require advanced training in SCADA systems, mobile devices, Cloud, and platforms/embedded systems. | Nil. |
|  | PO 001 – Conduct cyber reconnaissance: Cyber reconnaissance is a defined activity under Cyber Actions in the Cyber Operations JDN (para 0461 sub-para e). As a result, it is an appropriate performance statement for the PO. This PO sees the Red Analyst provided with references and tools necessary to establish and maintain situational awareness of the assigned target. This is achieved by conducting target profiling activities through the use of automated tools and manual methods. The training associated with this PO must provide the fundamental skills and knowledge required to progress through the MITRE ATT&CK matrix, including iterative cycles in support of further exploitation and post-exploitation phases. The board selected this matrix due to the comprehensive nature of the material, credibility of the source, and how readily it lends itself to defining the activities involved in the PO. | TPWB |
|  | PO 002 – Gain access to a target device or network environment: The term “abuse” included in this PO is a technical term in the information security community that refers to using software, hardware, or their configurations in a manner or method not intended by the owning party to achieve malicious goals. Building custom exploitation tools is a wide ranging task that can encompass tasks at a basic or extremely advanced level. This PO encompasses tasks at the intermediate level. The subject matter/technology associated with this PO will continually evolve. The information included in the TP should be written in such a way to minimize the requirement to update it regularly (e.g., generalize process descriptions where feasible, and reference to specific tools). | TPWB |
|  | PO 003 – Generate authorized adverse effects on target network devices:  This PO sees the Red Analyst provided with the references and tools necessary to generate authorized effects on network devices. This is achieved by employing any combination of the techniques included in the PO through the use of automated tools and manual methods.  The training associated with this PO must be scheduled before that for PO 004 as it will provide the fundamental skills and knowledge required to progress to those EOs.  The subject matter/technology associated with this PO will continually evolve. The information included in the TP should be written in such a way as to minimize the requirement to update it regularly (e.g., generalize process descriptions where feasible, and reference to specific tools). | TPWB |
|  | PO 004 – Conduct adversary specific cyber threat emulation: This PO represents a critical function of the Red Analyst, in that their knowledge, skill and experience in delivering offensive cyber effects are being utilized to train and evaluate the Blue Team. This is achieved by analysing existing adversarial tradecraft, and then emulating the derived actions against a defending Blue Team in a realistic cyber environment. This PO assumes that the Red Analyst has a strong foundation as a CYBER OP, and understands defensive cyber operations. This deep understanding of defensive cyber will allow the Red Analyst to effectively debrief the Blue Team, and allow the Red Analyst to mentor the Blue Analyst on how to effectively employ their defensive tools. Cyber threat emulation can be conducted on an exercise cyber range in support of an exercise, or it can be conducted on a production network in support of evaluating Blue Team detection and incident response. The range component requires a significant investment in planning and resources. To be effective, the range must emulate standard Internet services such as mail, DNS, and NTP. Furthermore, the range must have a Certificate Authority (CA), such that the Red Analyst can generate and employ signed SSL certificates. The range also needs to include user simulation. The TTPs for the specific named threat actor to be emulated shall be provided by the Intelligence section as a package, or derived by the Red Analyst using OSINT and, as required, classified sources. Cyber threat emulation is a fairly new concept; as such, there are limited references to draw on at this time. | TPWB |
|  | Qualified Red Analysts who have not been employed in a Red Team role for an extended period time (i.e., 12 months) will be required to complete refresher training and a practical assessment as/if required. | CFSCE/parent units |
|  | Senior Red Analysts will require more advanced training not reflected in this QSP in order to complete more specialized tasks (e.g., advanced exploitation development, mobile exploitation, and operational technology [OT] exploitation). | Employing units to develop PD plans for Snr Red Analysts. |
| **TRAINING PLAN DEVELOPMENT** |
|  | Instructional analysis began on Day 6 (23 Jul 18) and continued through to Day 13 (1 Aug 18). To inform this process, the course content for RMC’s Exploitation Techniques Course was reviewed for potential alignment with the skill and knowledge requirements for POs 001–004. Lesson specifications were completed on Day 13 (1 Aug 18), with the remainder of the time dedicated to completing the various chapters and annexes. The board completed the draft QSP on Day 15 (3 Aug 18). | Nil. |
|  | The board noted that candidates will need after hours access to the labs/training location in order to access the Internet for additional research and practice. | Instructional cadre to coordinate. |
|  | For PO 002, the board decided that, for the time being, the following software be employed as an attack framework:  Metasploit – general purpose penetration testing framework;  Cobalt Strike – Red Team and exploitation management framework; and  Power Shell Empire – Red Team and penetration testing framework. | CFSCE to coordinate software acquisition. |
|  | The board designed the POs to progress through the logical sequence of events leading to threat emulation/Red Teaming, with the series of performance checks outlined in Annex E (Assessment Plan) serving as “gates” that the candidate must successfully get through to enable them to move on to the next phase of training. | TE Stds |
|  | The board discussed the personnel resources required for training development, delivery and ongoing program maintenance. It was noted that CFSCE may not have sufficient qualified instructors to deliver this training. In order to support delivery, it was suggested that instructional staff be provided by the Red Team or other qualified personnel identified by the Red Team (via CFTPO). | TE/TA/RA |
|  | The board discussed the potential need for an on-job training (OJT) requirement. In order to develop higher levels of competency, it may be necessary for the Red Analyst to receive mentoring under a Snr Red Analyst. However, this may not be feasible for the pilot session given that units do not currently employ Red Analysts trained to the standards outlined in this QSP. Once a small cadre of Red Analysts is established, an OJT option may explored to hone positional-specific skills. | Nil. |
|  | The board discussed the required instructor qualifications. The CAF does not currently have a formalized process for training Red Analyst instructors. During the initial implementation phase of this course, it is unlikely that we will have available instructors who possess the Red Analyst qualification. To enable the pilot course to proceed, and to facilitate the PLAR process once the QSP is approved, the board recommended that a mix of operational experience and education such as a post-graduate attack theory course (e.g., RMC EE595) or demonstrated competency in Red Team techniques be considered as acceptable instructor backgrounds. | TE/TA/RA |
|  | The board discussed having a Blue Team present and actively defending an exercise network for the Red Analyst final PC. It was decided that this would be the best way to train and validate the candidates’ grasp of the course material, from actively scanning and exploiting network services, to Red Team operations and debriefing the Blue Team. | 4 Sqn CFSCE/CFNOC |
|  | The board discussed training all Blue Analysts on some Red Team roles. Rather than having Blue Analysts complete the formalized training developed in this QSP, it was suggested that tailored training be developed and delivered in a shorter timeframe. This tailored training would give some of the basics of the Red Analyst training to enhance the Blue Analyst’s capability on the job, and spark interest in some individuals to pursue the Red Analyst stream. | Future QSPWB consideration (i.e., MCpl RQ or Blue Analyst). |
|  | The board discussed lab development for the POs. Every effort should be made to create a realistic environment similar to those that would be seen on DND networks. | 4 Sqn CFSCE |
|  | The board discussed a need to implement a vetting process whereby candidates for this course would be carefully selected for nomination. Aptitude, interest and self-motivation are major factors that determine likelihood to succeed in this training. | Consideration for parent units/CYBER OP Career Manager |
|  | The “adhering to laws and policies impacting Red Team operations, to include the Criminal Code of Canada and the National Defence Act” aspect of EO 001.01 applies to ALL POs/EOs and instructors should make mention/remind candidates to keep this in mind at the beginning of every lesson. Policies also include DAODs – such as the acceptable use form they signed when they joined the course – and other classroom policies (e.g., authorized scope [“stay in the given cyber range” / ”don’t hack other candidates”]). | TE/instructional cadre |

